1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,955
[SQUEAKING]

2
00:00:01,955 --> 00:00:04,380
[RUSTLING]

3
00:00:04,380 --> 00:00:06,805
[CLICKING]

4
00:00:12,970 --> 00:00:14,220
SARA BEERY: Let's get started.

5
00:00:18,080 --> 00:00:22,520
So today, we're going to kick off the first of a little two

6
00:00:22,520 --> 00:00:25,520
lecture mini series on transfer learning,

7
00:00:25,520 --> 00:00:30,220
specifically how we think about taking information that

8
00:00:30,220 --> 00:00:31,720
might have been learned from a model

9
00:00:31,720 --> 00:00:37,420
and then transferring it to some new task, some new challenge,

10
00:00:37,420 --> 00:00:38,660
maybe some new distribution.

11
00:00:38,660 --> 00:00:42,440
We talked about distribution shifts last week.

12
00:00:42,440 --> 00:00:46,920
So at a high level, the idea of transfer learning

13
00:00:46,920 --> 00:00:51,000
is that you want to try to learn even if you

14
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:53,240
don't have very much data.

15
00:00:53,240 --> 00:00:57,040
And so we've tried to structure this framing of transfer

16
00:00:57,040 --> 00:01:00,272
learning into these different sub areas.

17
00:01:00,272 --> 00:01:02,480
So first, we're going to talk about how you can maybe

18
00:01:02,480 --> 00:01:05,319
transfer knowledge from a machine learning model

19
00:01:05,319 --> 00:01:08,880
that you have about the mapping from inputs to outputs.

20
00:01:08,880 --> 00:01:11,418
And so within that, we're going to talk about fine-tuning,

21
00:01:11,418 --> 00:01:12,960
and then we'll also talk a little bit

22
00:01:12,960 --> 00:01:15,960
about domain adaptation.

23
00:01:15,960 --> 00:01:17,960
And then we'll talk about transferring knowledge

24
00:01:17,960 --> 00:01:20,860
about the outputs, and so in that case,

25
00:01:20,860 --> 00:01:23,522
we're going to be discussing knowledge distillation.

26
00:01:23,522 --> 00:01:25,480
And then we're going to be talking a little bit

27
00:01:25,480 --> 00:01:29,040
about foundation models and maybe what this new paradigm is

28
00:01:29,040 --> 00:01:31,920
in terms of transfer learning, kind of introducing

29
00:01:31,920 --> 00:01:35,200
this idea of in-context learning or prompting

30
00:01:35,200 --> 00:01:39,560
as a mechanism for transfer learning by just asking.

31
00:01:39,560 --> 00:01:43,920
And then next Tuesday, we're going to finish this up

32
00:01:43,920 --> 00:01:46,960
with one further discussion of how we might transfer knowledge

33
00:01:46,960 --> 00:01:49,680
about the inputs of the data.

34
00:01:49,680 --> 00:01:53,820
So why deep learning?

35
00:01:53,820 --> 00:01:56,040
This is a slide from Andrew Ng, who, of course,

36
00:01:56,040 --> 00:02:01,280
I think has a very, very famous lecture series on deep learning.

37
00:02:01,280 --> 00:02:05,080
And here, he essentially is pointing out

38
00:02:05,080 --> 00:02:08,680
that deep learning might be different from older

39
00:02:08,680 --> 00:02:12,840
or standard learning algorithms specifically in terms

40
00:02:12,840 --> 00:02:15,080
of how it scales with the amount of data

41
00:02:15,080 --> 00:02:16,580
that you have to train on.

42
00:02:16,580 --> 00:02:19,480
It might be a little bit hard to see with the lights as they are.

43
00:02:19,480 --> 00:02:23,380
But essentially, the idea is that many learning algorithms,

44
00:02:23,380 --> 00:02:25,343
older learning algorithms, saturate.

45
00:02:25,343 --> 00:02:26,760
With some amount of data, you just

46
00:02:26,760 --> 00:02:29,200
don't see performance continuing to improve.

47
00:02:29,200 --> 00:02:34,120
But we have really not seen that type of saturation

48
00:02:34,120 --> 00:02:36,400
as we scale the amount of data that we're giving

49
00:02:36,400 --> 00:02:38,400
these deep learning algorithms.

50
00:02:38,400 --> 00:02:41,000
And next Thursday, you guys will have a lecture

51
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:42,800
from Phil around some of these scaling

52
00:02:42,800 --> 00:02:45,000
laws, these relationships that people

53
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:47,160
are starting to learn about the scale

54
00:02:47,160 --> 00:02:50,560
of the number of parameters, the amount of available data.

55
00:02:50,560 --> 00:02:59,270
But one kind of high level analysis that was done

56
00:02:59,270 --> 00:03:02,030
seemed to suggest that with the current capacity of machine

57
00:03:02,030 --> 00:03:08,950
learning models we have, we basically have far more capacity

58
00:03:08,950 --> 00:03:10,030
than data.

59
00:03:10,030 --> 00:03:13,670
So essentially, the idea that there's a lot more room

60
00:03:13,670 --> 00:03:15,970
to scale on the data dimension still

61
00:03:15,970 --> 00:03:18,430
before we're really hitting the capacity of our largest

62
00:03:18,430 --> 00:03:20,830
deep learning models.

63
00:03:20,830 --> 00:03:25,070
And that's talking about billion data point data sets, things

64
00:03:25,070 --> 00:03:27,790
that are on the order of scale of the entire internet.

65
00:03:27,790 --> 00:03:31,710
We're still not hitting the actual computational capacity

66
00:03:31,710 --> 00:03:32,770
of these models.

67
00:03:35,310 --> 00:03:41,190
So few-shot learning is the idea that you can maybe learn even

68
00:03:41,190 --> 00:03:43,990
if you have very little data.

69
00:03:43,990 --> 00:03:47,670
So it turns out that humans are shockingly good at this.

70
00:03:47,670 --> 00:03:51,310
We're really good at recognizing patterns and translating them

71
00:03:51,310 --> 00:03:54,030
even to things that we've never seen.

72
00:03:54,030 --> 00:03:58,750
So here, if I tell you that symbol up at the top is a dax--

73
00:03:58,750 --> 00:03:59,670
these are made up.

74
00:03:59,670 --> 00:04:00,490
This isn't real.

75
00:04:00,490 --> 00:04:02,550
But if I tell you that's a dax, can you guys

76
00:04:02,550 --> 00:04:05,250
tell me which of the ones below is also a dax?

77
00:04:09,110 --> 00:04:09,870
Yeah.

78
00:04:09,870 --> 00:04:12,330
AUDIENCE: Second row, second from the right?

79
00:04:12,330 --> 00:04:13,530
SARA BEERY: Second row, second from the right.

80
00:04:13,530 --> 00:04:14,010
Yeah.

81
00:04:14,010 --> 00:04:14,510
Right?

82
00:04:14,510 --> 00:04:17,630
It looks like a dax.

83
00:04:17,630 --> 00:04:19,029
OK.

84
00:04:19,029 --> 00:04:20,829
What about this?

85
00:04:20,829 --> 00:04:24,950
Which on the bottom is an example of the same concept

86
00:04:24,950 --> 00:04:26,470
as the item in the box?

87
00:04:26,470 --> 00:04:27,330
Someone else.

88
00:04:30,390 --> 00:04:31,070
Yeah.

89
00:04:31,070 --> 00:04:34,243
AUDIENCE: Second row, first to the right.

90
00:04:34,243 --> 00:04:34,910
SARA BEERY: Yes.

91
00:04:34,910 --> 00:04:36,970
So those are both Segways, right?

92
00:04:36,970 --> 00:04:41,110
But then this kind of begs this interesting question,

93
00:04:41,110 --> 00:04:44,970
because there's potentially ambiguity here.

94
00:04:44,970 --> 00:04:46,770
If you only have this single example,

95
00:04:46,770 --> 00:04:49,510
and you're telling me which is the same, what you made

96
00:04:49,510 --> 00:04:51,630
was the guess that was probably most

97
00:04:51,630 --> 00:04:55,590
similar because the orientation of the wheels and the structure.

98
00:04:55,590 --> 00:04:59,170
But maybe the thing I was looking for was instead,

99
00:04:59,170 --> 00:05:00,590
actually, I wanted you to tell me

100
00:05:00,590 --> 00:05:03,570
all the things that had two wheels instead of four or one.

101
00:05:03,570 --> 00:05:05,910
And so one of the things that's challenging when you're

102
00:05:05,910 --> 00:05:09,990
doing this few-shot learning type of scenario

103
00:05:09,990 --> 00:05:12,830
is with less data, it can be harder

104
00:05:12,830 --> 00:05:15,110
to understand without ambiguity what

105
00:05:15,110 --> 00:05:18,590
the boundaries of maybe the categories of interest are.

106
00:05:18,590 --> 00:05:22,710
So if I'd showed you three examples, one was a Segway,

107
00:05:22,710 --> 00:05:25,090
one was a scooter, and one was a bicycle,

108
00:05:25,090 --> 00:05:28,730
and I said, OK, find me the ones that are also in this category,

109
00:05:28,730 --> 00:05:31,850
it would have been much easier for you to understand,

110
00:05:31,850 --> 00:05:33,990
OK, she's looking for things with two wheels.

111
00:05:33,990 --> 00:05:36,305
Or at some different level of granularity,

112
00:05:36,305 --> 00:05:38,930
maybe I was trying to just learn wheeled things, in which case,

113
00:05:38,930 --> 00:05:41,222
I might need to have, again, more diverse examples that

114
00:05:41,222 --> 00:05:44,710
cover the space of the concept I want to learn.

115
00:05:44,710 --> 00:05:49,190
So few-shot learning really is just this idea

116
00:05:49,190 --> 00:05:54,150
that we can learn to maybe generalize

117
00:05:54,150 --> 00:05:59,630
from our past lived experience how to recognize similarity

118
00:05:59,630 --> 00:06:03,390
and patterns in a useful way.

119
00:06:03,390 --> 00:06:06,910
So up until this point, a lot of what

120
00:06:06,910 --> 00:06:09,468
we've talked about with deep learning

121
00:06:09,468 --> 00:06:11,510
is, essentially, you're going to have a data set,

122
00:06:11,510 --> 00:06:14,253
and you start with an empty slate.

123
00:06:14,253 --> 00:06:15,670
And then you're essentially trying

124
00:06:15,670 --> 00:06:20,110
to learn some physical rules, some concepts, some dimensions

125
00:06:20,110 --> 00:06:24,910
of similarity that are useful for a specific task.

126
00:06:24,910 --> 00:06:31,030
But what humans do instead, when they try to learn something new,

127
00:06:31,030 --> 00:06:34,430
is we really have a lot of prior knowledge coming

128
00:06:34,430 --> 00:06:37,550
from our experience in the world.

129
00:06:37,550 --> 00:06:39,990
From the time that we're incredibly young,

130
00:06:39,990 --> 00:06:43,050
we're physically embodied in the world,

131
00:06:43,050 --> 00:06:46,630
learning about concepts like physics, like structure.

132
00:06:46,630 --> 00:06:49,110
We're learning through the entirety of our education

133
00:06:49,110 --> 00:06:50,960
and just in our experience, in our lives,

134
00:06:50,960 --> 00:06:53,700
how to think about these measures of similarity

135
00:06:53,700 --> 00:06:54,780
and difference.

136
00:06:54,780 --> 00:06:58,260
And different people can have different mental models

137
00:06:58,260 --> 00:06:59,040
of the world.

138
00:06:59,040 --> 00:07:01,980
This actually comes up a lot currently

139
00:07:01,980 --> 00:07:04,100
when you're thinking about scaling large scale

140
00:07:04,100 --> 00:07:08,540
data labeling, that, actually, if you're getting data labeled

141
00:07:08,540 --> 00:07:10,420
from people from different cultures,

142
00:07:10,420 --> 00:07:12,420
they might actually have different mental models

143
00:07:12,420 --> 00:07:14,480
than the one that you intend in your culture.

144
00:07:14,480 --> 00:07:17,660
So there's this idea of, my mental model

145
00:07:17,660 --> 00:07:20,500
is based on some prior, and we don't necessarily know--

146
00:07:20,500 --> 00:07:23,060
or there really isn't a concept of which mental model

147
00:07:23,060 --> 00:07:25,820
of the world is correct.

148
00:07:25,820 --> 00:07:27,900
But these mental models are built,

149
00:07:27,900 --> 00:07:30,660
and they kind of capture representations

150
00:07:30,660 --> 00:07:34,020
of things, kind of models we have of how the world operates,

151
00:07:34,020 --> 00:07:35,700
maybe different skills.

152
00:07:35,700 --> 00:07:39,180
And that means that we're basing anything

153
00:07:39,180 --> 00:07:42,360
we learn on top of a bunch of prior knowledge,

154
00:07:42,360 --> 00:07:44,020
basically like systems that are really

155
00:07:44,020 --> 00:07:46,335
well posed to learn new things.

156
00:07:46,335 --> 00:07:48,460
And actually, a lot of what you're doing in college

157
00:07:48,460 --> 00:07:50,620
is learning how to learn.

158
00:07:50,620 --> 00:07:53,820
You're learning how to learn new things efficiently.

159
00:07:53,820 --> 00:07:57,540
And so there's this question of, how can we

160
00:07:57,540 --> 00:08:00,020
give deep learning models or deep networks

161
00:08:00,020 --> 00:08:03,500
similar access to prior knowledge?

162
00:08:03,500 --> 00:08:06,620
And so a very, very simple way to do that

163
00:08:06,620 --> 00:08:11,960
is just pretrain them on other tasks or on prior tasks.

164
00:08:11,960 --> 00:08:16,020
So the idea that you can send your neural network to school,

165
00:08:16,020 --> 00:08:19,003
and you can try to get it to learn how to learn,

166
00:08:19,003 --> 00:08:20,420
and the way that you might do that

167
00:08:20,420 --> 00:08:23,940
is maybe training on a huge amount of data

168
00:08:23,940 --> 00:08:26,020
with some other set of categories that might not

169
00:08:26,020 --> 00:08:28,187
be the thing you're really explicitly interested in,

170
00:08:28,187 --> 00:08:31,700
but it's things that you have data for at scale.

171
00:08:31,700 --> 00:08:33,659
And then you can use some mechanism

172
00:08:33,659 --> 00:08:35,740
to transfer that knowledge from the pretained net

173
00:08:35,740 --> 00:08:38,140
so that you can solve new tasks.

174
00:08:38,140 --> 00:08:41,700
And so one view of this idea of learning

175
00:08:41,700 --> 00:08:43,460
from little data or transfer learning

176
00:08:43,460 --> 00:08:45,580
is actually the point of deep learning

177
00:08:45,580 --> 00:08:48,820
is to enable us to be able to do this type of problem solving

178
00:08:48,820 --> 00:08:50,260
with little data.

179
00:08:50,260 --> 00:08:52,980
So you're trying to learn these representations,

180
00:08:52,980 --> 00:08:57,080
these models that have a good sense of what's useful.

181
00:08:57,080 --> 00:09:01,420
What is a good thing to build on top of if we want to learn?

182
00:09:01,420 --> 00:09:03,500
So that's kind of the motivation for talking

183
00:09:03,500 --> 00:09:06,420
about transfer learning, right?

184
00:09:06,420 --> 00:09:10,140
So when we think about a model in general,

185
00:09:10,140 --> 00:09:12,340
usually, we have some big system,

186
00:09:12,340 --> 00:09:13,780
and it will map some set of inputs

187
00:09:13,780 --> 00:09:18,420
to some set of outputs in some very fun, complex, nonlinear

188
00:09:18,420 --> 00:09:21,060
way.

189
00:09:21,060 --> 00:09:26,640
But we're thinking about, what actually are we transferring?

190
00:09:26,640 --> 00:09:30,420
And so you can think about transferring

191
00:09:30,420 --> 00:09:33,800
some knowledge about the relationship, for example,

192
00:09:33,800 --> 00:09:35,658
from the inputs to the outputs.

193
00:09:35,658 --> 00:09:37,700
You could also think about transferring knowledge

194
00:09:37,700 --> 00:09:38,520
in different ways.

195
00:09:38,520 --> 00:09:40,340
We can think about transferring knowledge

196
00:09:40,340 --> 00:09:42,640
about biases in a model.

197
00:09:42,640 --> 00:09:45,880
You can think about transferring knowledge about an optimizer,

198
00:09:45,880 --> 00:09:46,940
perhaps.

199
00:09:46,940 --> 00:09:49,540
How do we optimize models?

200
00:09:49,540 --> 00:09:51,900
We can think about maybe transferring knowledge

201
00:09:51,900 --> 00:09:55,780
about potential error modes or ethical risks

202
00:09:55,780 --> 00:09:58,700
or societal implications of models.

203
00:09:58,700 --> 00:10:02,380
So we'll really be focusing on pretty simple mechanisms

204
00:10:02,380 --> 00:10:04,140
to think about knowledge transfer,

205
00:10:04,140 --> 00:10:06,180
but I do think that there's a lot of more

206
00:10:06,180 --> 00:10:11,780
broad sets of transfer or this idea of sharing information

207
00:10:11,780 --> 00:10:14,540
in a valuable way that are really not understood

208
00:10:14,540 --> 00:10:15,320
or not solved.

209
00:10:15,320 --> 00:10:19,300
So one thing that we still haven't really

210
00:10:19,300 --> 00:10:22,380
figured out how to do is building

211
00:10:22,380 --> 00:10:27,020
an agent that's able to actually learn some intrinsic motivation

212
00:10:27,020 --> 00:10:30,540
about how to explore in the world, which then could be

213
00:10:30,540 --> 00:10:33,140
transferred to a new agent, which would let them explore

214
00:10:33,140 --> 00:10:33,880
more efficiently.

215
00:10:33,880 --> 00:10:37,140
This idea of transferring motivation

216
00:10:37,140 --> 00:10:38,740
within a robotic agent.

217
00:10:38,740 --> 00:10:41,500
So there's a lot of ways that we have

218
00:10:41,500 --> 00:10:45,380
these kind of brute force knowledge transfer mechanisms

219
00:10:45,380 --> 00:10:46,600
that we'll talk about.

220
00:10:46,600 --> 00:10:48,540
But I think there's a lot of space

221
00:10:48,540 --> 00:10:51,700
still to explore in the machine learning research arena, just

222
00:10:51,700 --> 00:10:54,820
in terms of the best ways to try to share information

223
00:10:54,820 --> 00:10:56,260
and transfer knowledge.

224
00:10:56,260 --> 00:10:57,060
Yeah?

225
00:10:57,060 --> 00:11:00,260
AUDIENCE: How much of that is limited--

226
00:11:00,260 --> 00:11:04,100
my very basic understanding of some of the challenges in, say,

227
00:11:04,100 --> 00:11:06,860
reinforcement learning is that it's

228
00:11:06,860 --> 00:11:09,100
more of an issue of sample efficiency.

229
00:11:09,100 --> 00:11:12,900
And thinking back to 2012, one of the breakthroughs

230
00:11:12,900 --> 00:11:14,800
wasn't really that the algorithms changed,

231
00:11:14,800 --> 00:11:17,900
but just that they finally got it [INAUDIBLE] GPU,

232
00:11:17,900 --> 00:11:21,260
and it sped up the training process.

233
00:11:21,260 --> 00:11:24,460
How much do people think that the bottleneck

234
00:11:24,460 --> 00:11:27,300
is the algorithm or the ideas around how

235
00:11:27,300 --> 00:11:30,060
to do this versus just we're not computationally

236
00:11:30,060 --> 00:11:31,932
efficient enough?

237
00:11:31,932 --> 00:11:33,640
SARA BEERY: So the question is basically,

238
00:11:33,640 --> 00:11:35,848
what's the bottleneck in terms of knowledge transfer?

239
00:11:35,848 --> 00:11:37,860
Is it fundamentally algorithmic, or might it

240
00:11:37,860 --> 00:11:42,110
be related to, instead, computational efficiency?

241
00:11:42,110 --> 00:11:44,610
And you brought up a really great example of essentially

242
00:11:44,610 --> 00:11:48,170
how figuring out how to use GPUs effectively

243
00:11:48,170 --> 00:11:50,112
was a huge breakthrough in terms of progress

244
00:11:50,112 --> 00:11:52,570
across many different areas of deep learning, reinforcement

245
00:11:52,570 --> 00:11:56,930
learning being one of them, but basically anything else as well.

246
00:11:56,930 --> 00:11:58,670
I think it's a little bit of everything.

247
00:11:58,670 --> 00:12:00,590
And this question of sample efficiency,

248
00:12:00,590 --> 00:12:05,010
I would not say that we have good algorithms

249
00:12:05,010 --> 00:12:08,130
for sampling data efficiently or for learning

250
00:12:08,130 --> 00:12:09,990
from that sample data efficiently.

251
00:12:09,990 --> 00:12:11,950
I mean, we have algorithms that do it,

252
00:12:11,950 --> 00:12:16,130
but I feel like there's a lot of room to improve.

253
00:12:16,130 --> 00:12:17,210
Cool.

254
00:12:17,210 --> 00:12:19,050
So we're first going to talk a little bit

255
00:12:19,050 --> 00:12:22,202
about this idea of transferring knowledge from the mapping,

256
00:12:22,202 --> 00:12:24,410
so essentially, transferring knowledge from the model

257
00:12:24,410 --> 00:12:25,350
you've learned itself.

258
00:12:25,350 --> 00:12:28,930
How you go from inputs to useful outputs?

259
00:12:28,930 --> 00:12:32,630
So a simple example of this would be something like, OK.

260
00:12:32,630 --> 00:12:39,050
I have a lot of data on music with associated genres,

261
00:12:39,050 --> 00:12:44,450
and the idea being that being able to recognize a genre

262
00:12:44,450 --> 00:12:46,450
is potentially useful.

263
00:12:46,450 --> 00:12:49,090
It's teaching models something about the structure

264
00:12:49,090 --> 00:12:52,593
of music, something about what makes different songs similar.

265
00:12:52,593 --> 00:12:54,010
Two things being of the same genre

266
00:12:54,010 --> 00:12:56,530
argues that they might be similar in some way.

267
00:12:56,530 --> 00:13:00,090
And then maybe what you want to then adapt or transfer

268
00:13:00,090 --> 00:13:01,930
that knowledge to is something where

269
00:13:01,930 --> 00:13:05,050
you have a lot less data that specifically predicting,

270
00:13:05,050 --> 00:13:08,690
let's say, your individual preferences for songs.

271
00:13:08,690 --> 00:13:11,570
So maybe you can do genre recognition

272
00:13:11,570 --> 00:13:15,010
on the entire cadre of music on Spotify.

273
00:13:15,010 --> 00:13:18,570
And then if you actually want to learn

274
00:13:18,570 --> 00:13:21,590
how to predict what songs you're likely to enjoy,

275
00:13:21,590 --> 00:13:23,650
that you're going to prefer, you might only

276
00:13:23,650 --> 00:13:25,382
have a much smaller set of data, which

277
00:13:25,382 --> 00:13:27,090
is all the songs you've ever listened to,

278
00:13:27,090 --> 00:13:30,090
which is, of course, not even close to all the songs

279
00:13:30,090 --> 00:13:31,890
that exist.

280
00:13:31,890 --> 00:13:35,250
And then the actual testing would then be taking

281
00:13:35,250 --> 00:13:39,250
of fine-tuned model-- you can see they have this f prime--

282
00:13:39,250 --> 00:13:41,373
and using it to actually predict on new music

283
00:13:41,373 --> 00:13:42,790
whether you're going to it or not.

284
00:13:42,790 --> 00:13:44,957
And then-- I don't know-- see, do you like the music

285
00:13:44,957 --> 00:13:47,490
that it's suggesting for you?

286
00:13:47,490 --> 00:13:51,930
And so this is kind of that broad, simple thing in terms

287
00:13:51,930 --> 00:13:54,850
of what we call fine-tuning.

288
00:13:54,850 --> 00:13:57,730
And at a very high level, it's that you're

289
00:13:57,730 --> 00:14:01,010
pretaining your network, your set of weights and biases,

290
00:14:01,010 --> 00:14:04,930
on some task that's a pretaining task.

291
00:14:04,930 --> 00:14:07,730
And that will result in some parameters for your model, W

292
00:14:07,730 --> 00:14:09,530
and b, weights and biases.

293
00:14:09,530 --> 00:14:11,530
Then you initialize a second network

294
00:14:11,530 --> 00:14:15,130
with some or all of W and b, and so there's

295
00:14:15,130 --> 00:14:18,090
these kind of interesting question

296
00:14:18,090 --> 00:14:22,770
of how much knowledge about the mapping you want to transfer.

297
00:14:22,770 --> 00:14:25,970
So usually, maybe the very final layer of a model, that's

298
00:14:25,970 --> 00:14:29,090
going to be pretty specific to the pretaining task.

299
00:14:29,090 --> 00:14:31,810
So often, it doesn't really make sense, especially

300
00:14:31,810 --> 00:14:34,730
if the output shape is not the same.

301
00:14:34,730 --> 00:14:38,970
You cut that off, you add some different final layer on,

302
00:14:38,970 --> 00:14:42,850
but maybe, you really only want those early layer features

303
00:14:42,850 --> 00:14:43,630
to be captured.

304
00:14:43,630 --> 00:14:45,790
And so you actually let the model--

305
00:14:45,790 --> 00:14:51,490
you initialize it with less of the initial model.

306
00:14:51,490 --> 00:14:54,290
And then you think about training that second network

307
00:14:54,290 --> 00:14:55,835
on some task b.

308
00:14:55,835 --> 00:14:58,210
And so now, you'll get some slightly adjusted parameters,

309
00:14:58,210 --> 00:15:00,010
W prime and b prime.

310
00:15:00,010 --> 00:15:07,410
And the idea is then you have this learned representation that

311
00:15:07,410 --> 00:15:10,990
is essentially an encoder, and that's what we're transferring.

312
00:15:10,990 --> 00:15:13,570
So you're taking the representation learned

313
00:15:13,570 --> 00:15:16,690
from the initial model that's encoding something

314
00:15:16,690 --> 00:15:20,810
useful about your data, and then you're transferring that

315
00:15:20,810 --> 00:15:22,610
and then learning how to make use

316
00:15:22,610 --> 00:15:25,650
of that useful representation on something downstream.

317
00:15:25,650 --> 00:15:27,690
And we'll talk a little bit about some

318
00:15:27,690 --> 00:15:30,370
of these different empirical choices

319
00:15:30,370 --> 00:15:33,490
you can make here around how much you

320
00:15:33,490 --> 00:15:37,370
let the model adapt, how much you

321
00:15:37,370 --> 00:15:39,610
let it change from the initial representation.

322
00:15:39,610 --> 00:15:42,770
But empirically, actually, as long as the types of input data

323
00:15:42,770 --> 00:15:44,470
are similar-- so images and images,

324
00:15:44,470 --> 00:15:47,130
for example-- what we often find is

325
00:15:47,130 --> 00:15:50,155
that a lot of the structure in the models,

326
00:15:50,155 --> 00:15:51,530
like weights and biases, actually

327
00:15:51,530 --> 00:15:54,690
stays the same, particularly in those early layers

328
00:15:54,690 --> 00:15:56,410
after you've done the fine-tuning.

329
00:15:56,410 --> 00:15:58,890
So even if you haven't explicitly constrained the model

330
00:15:58,890 --> 00:16:01,490
not to change that very much, what you can do

331
00:16:01,490 --> 00:16:04,507
is keep a learning rate low or freeze some of the network.

332
00:16:04,507 --> 00:16:06,090
But if you don't do that, you actually

333
00:16:06,090 --> 00:16:08,050
find that a lot of the structure will

334
00:16:08,050 --> 00:16:10,810
stay the same, which does seem to argue that some of that

335
00:16:10,810 --> 00:16:12,750
is just universal in some sense.

336
00:16:12,750 --> 00:16:19,050
It's universally useful for, let's say, images or audio.

337
00:16:19,050 --> 00:16:22,633
So we previously saw a very specific case

338
00:16:22,633 --> 00:16:25,050
when we were talking about similarity-based representation

339
00:16:25,050 --> 00:16:28,670
learning where we talked about self-supervised pretaining.

340
00:16:28,670 --> 00:16:32,790
So here, with this self-supervised pretaining,

341
00:16:32,790 --> 00:16:36,680
we're not necessarily explicitly or directly training

342
00:16:36,680 --> 00:16:38,960
the model to do something we might care about.

343
00:16:38,960 --> 00:16:41,880
Instead, we're just training the model to learn something

344
00:16:41,880 --> 00:16:44,400
about data similarity.

345
00:16:44,400 --> 00:16:46,040
And then that ends up--

346
00:16:46,040 --> 00:16:48,960
it has been shown-- to be quite useful for training

347
00:16:48,960 --> 00:16:51,440
representations that are good for downstream tasks we might

348
00:16:51,440 --> 00:16:52,052
care about.

349
00:16:52,052 --> 00:16:53,760
And one of the main benefits of this type

350
00:16:53,760 --> 00:16:56,040
of self-supervised pretaining is the fact

351
00:16:56,040 --> 00:17:00,200
that, because it doesn't require labels for data,

352
00:17:00,200 --> 00:17:04,579
it's making that pretaining on a lot of data-- like a lot, a lot,

353
00:17:04,579 --> 00:17:05,440
a lot of data.

354
00:17:05,440 --> 00:17:08,560
The amount of data that exists without any specific label

355
00:17:08,560 --> 00:17:13,040
is much larger than any of labeled or clean data set.

356
00:17:13,040 --> 00:17:16,523
So here, you think about contrastive pretaining

357
00:17:16,523 --> 00:17:18,440
and then maybe you're going to test it on some

358
00:17:18,440 --> 00:17:21,119
of new recognition task.

359
00:17:21,119 --> 00:17:25,325
But instead, you could think about fine-tuning a model, which

360
00:17:25,325 --> 00:17:27,200
is how you take those initial representations

361
00:17:27,200 --> 00:17:29,440
and transfer them to then actually learn

362
00:17:29,440 --> 00:17:32,280
how to do that new recognition task.

363
00:17:32,280 --> 00:17:32,815
OK.

364
00:17:32,815 --> 00:17:35,440
So now, I'm going to get into a couple pretty simple but pretty

365
00:17:35,440 --> 00:17:38,400
practical considerations.

366
00:17:38,400 --> 00:17:43,560
So what if you're pretaining on some images,

367
00:17:43,560 --> 00:17:46,360
and you're predicting whether they're wheat or corn?

368
00:17:46,360 --> 00:17:52,080
And then now, we want to fine tune our model.

369
00:17:52,080 --> 00:17:57,360
But now, we have an x prime and a y prime that are not the same.

370
00:17:57,360 --> 00:17:59,960
So essentially, we have a model, and our initial model

371
00:17:59,960 --> 00:18:02,760
was trained on images that are RGB.

372
00:18:02,760 --> 00:18:06,560
And it was trained to predict two categories as the output.

373
00:18:06,560 --> 00:18:08,720
And now, the model we actually want

374
00:18:08,720 --> 00:18:12,720
is going to actually take in images that are just grayscale.

375
00:18:12,720 --> 00:18:14,840
So we only have a single dimension,

376
00:18:14,840 --> 00:18:18,760
and we want to predict on outputs that

377
00:18:18,760 --> 00:18:21,160
have an additional category.

378
00:18:21,160 --> 00:18:23,440
So the structure of the model itself

379
00:18:23,440 --> 00:18:25,560
is actually a bit different.

380
00:18:25,560 --> 00:18:27,360
Essentially, if you're changing either

381
00:18:27,360 --> 00:18:30,920
your input dimension or your output dimension or both.

382
00:18:30,920 --> 00:18:36,480
So one kind of very simple mechanism that you can do

383
00:18:36,480 --> 00:18:38,800
is you can add kind of like a glue

384
00:18:38,800 --> 00:18:43,400
into the model, which is just a way

385
00:18:43,400 --> 00:18:47,200
to transfer data through to get it into the right format.

386
00:18:47,200 --> 00:18:48,980
So then when you're doing your pretaining,

387
00:18:48,980 --> 00:18:50,840
if you know that your downstream task is not

388
00:18:50,840 --> 00:18:54,040
going to have necessarily the same structure,

389
00:18:54,040 --> 00:18:58,400
you define these new z's, which are maybe models,

390
00:18:58,400 --> 00:19:02,180
or even just like a few simple layers, fully connected layers,

391
00:19:02,180 --> 00:19:05,280
what have you, that enable us to then

392
00:19:05,280 --> 00:19:09,880
map each input and output to the same dimensionality.

393
00:19:09,880 --> 00:19:12,120
And so you would do this where you need it, right?

394
00:19:12,120 --> 00:19:14,680
If you still are going to have RGB images,

395
00:19:14,680 --> 00:19:17,720
but then all you're doing is adding one more output

396
00:19:17,720 --> 00:19:20,360
dimension, well, then one thing you can do

397
00:19:20,360 --> 00:19:23,940
is you can take the final output layer of your initial model,

398
00:19:23,940 --> 00:19:26,080
and you can learn some reprojection to project it

399
00:19:26,080 --> 00:19:27,220
into more dimensions.

400
00:19:27,220 --> 00:19:29,080
Or you can do what I talked about sooner,

401
00:19:29,080 --> 00:19:32,280
and you can consider this z to be

402
00:19:32,280 --> 00:19:35,120
like that final mapping from your representation,

403
00:19:35,120 --> 00:19:38,380
that encoder, to the number of categories you want to predict.

404
00:19:38,380 --> 00:19:41,000
And then you can just swap that out and maybe learn

405
00:19:41,000 --> 00:19:44,740
some different one that's now in this new model.

406
00:19:44,740 --> 00:19:46,452
So you're just essentially defining

407
00:19:46,452 --> 00:19:48,160
points within the structure of your model

408
00:19:48,160 --> 00:19:50,040
that give you a bit of flexibility.

409
00:19:50,040 --> 00:19:52,900
So now, this f1 goes to f1 prime.

410
00:19:52,900 --> 00:19:54,600
Those will be different from each other.

411
00:19:54,600 --> 00:19:55,535
f3 goes to f3 prime.

412
00:19:55,535 --> 00:19:57,160
Those will be different from each other

413
00:19:57,160 --> 00:19:59,285
in terms of their structure, like the actual number

414
00:19:59,285 --> 00:20:01,720
of layers, weights, or size.

415
00:20:01,720 --> 00:20:03,680
But then the f2 can stay the same.

416
00:20:03,680 --> 00:20:05,500
So the vast majority of the structure,

417
00:20:05,500 --> 00:20:08,480
you're not actually changing.

418
00:20:08,480 --> 00:20:11,160
And this actually comes up quite a lot,

419
00:20:11,160 --> 00:20:14,360
particularly for some types of data that have maybe more input

420
00:20:14,360 --> 00:20:15,180
dimensions.

421
00:20:15,180 --> 00:20:17,840
So most image models are pretained

422
00:20:17,840 --> 00:20:21,240
on things like ImageNet, or these days, LAION,

423
00:20:21,240 --> 00:20:23,830
where you have huge amounts of RGB data.

424
00:20:23,830 --> 00:20:26,080
But then say you're someone who's working in robotics,

425
00:20:26,080 --> 00:20:30,950
and you have RGB-D. You have this additional depth dimension.

426
00:20:30,950 --> 00:20:34,830
It's often true that you can still get a lot of value

427
00:20:34,830 --> 00:20:39,270
in doing transfer learning from models trained without depth,

428
00:20:39,270 --> 00:20:42,550
but now, you have to figure out how to add this additional depth

429
00:20:42,550 --> 00:20:44,935
dimension during your fine-tuning effectively.

430
00:20:44,935 --> 00:20:46,310
And so often, the way people will

431
00:20:46,310 --> 00:20:49,710
do that is they just learn some of additional encoding

432
00:20:49,710 --> 00:20:52,450
that takes that and maps it into three dimensions.

433
00:20:52,450 --> 00:20:54,450
Or sometimes, depending on how much data

434
00:20:54,450 --> 00:20:56,950
you have, whether you can really learn that type of mapping,

435
00:20:56,950 --> 00:21:02,310
you do some of strange, just very simple model

436
00:21:02,310 --> 00:21:05,090
of how to transform one input dimension into another.

437
00:21:05,090 --> 00:21:07,750
So if you're working with very high scale hyperspectral

438
00:21:07,750 --> 00:21:09,630
satellite data, and you have models

439
00:21:09,630 --> 00:21:11,890
that are pretained with three channel inputs,

440
00:21:11,890 --> 00:21:14,990
sometimes, people will just run something like PCA

441
00:21:14,990 --> 00:21:19,590
and take the principal components, the three most

442
00:21:19,590 --> 00:21:21,190
informative principal components,

443
00:21:21,190 --> 00:21:23,282
over all those hyperspectral bands,

444
00:21:23,282 --> 00:21:24,990
and use that as their three channel input

445
00:21:24,990 --> 00:21:29,870
when they're fine-tuning because the ability to actually learn

446
00:21:29,870 --> 00:21:33,350
all of those 380 different input layers effectively is,

447
00:21:33,350 --> 00:21:36,485
one, computationally very expensive, and two,

448
00:21:36,485 --> 00:21:38,110
if you don't have a lot of data, that's

449
00:21:38,110 --> 00:21:39,610
a lot of parameters to learn.

450
00:21:39,610 --> 00:21:42,030
And so that can be tricky.

451
00:21:42,030 --> 00:21:42,830
Yeah.

452
00:21:42,830 --> 00:21:45,550
AUDIENCE: So we're talking about adding or changing

453
00:21:45,550 --> 00:21:48,270
the stacks and the [INAUDIBLE].

454
00:21:48,270 --> 00:21:51,550
Do you ever add stuff into this dimension for fine-tuning,

455
00:21:51,550 --> 00:21:53,227
or is that just--

456
00:21:53,227 --> 00:21:54,310
SARA BEERY: In the width--

457
00:21:54,310 --> 00:21:56,310
so the input size would change?

458
00:21:56,310 --> 00:21:59,710
AUDIENCE: Basically, say if you had f2,

459
00:21:59,710 --> 00:22:05,070
adding additional channels to be parallel to f2.

460
00:22:05,070 --> 00:22:06,550
SARA BEERY: Like concatenating?

461
00:22:06,550 --> 00:22:07,402
AUDIENCE: Kind of.

462
00:22:07,402 --> 00:22:08,110
SARA BEERY: Yeah.

463
00:22:08,110 --> 00:22:10,990
I mean, so definitely, a lot of these models

464
00:22:10,990 --> 00:22:13,230
will assume some of fixed input size.

465
00:22:13,230 --> 00:22:15,750
And then, say, maybe you want to train the model on a bigger

466
00:22:15,750 --> 00:22:16,408
input size.

467
00:22:16,408 --> 00:22:17,950
That's another way that you can think

468
00:22:17,950 --> 00:22:19,990
about building this little f1 that

469
00:22:19,990 --> 00:22:22,070
will take a larger input size image

470
00:22:22,070 --> 00:22:24,710
and then learn how to get it to that smaller input size

471
00:22:24,710 --> 00:22:28,350
that the pretained model was trained on.

472
00:22:28,350 --> 00:22:29,833
But I think the only way to think

473
00:22:29,833 --> 00:22:31,750
about changing the width dimension is in terms

474
00:22:31,750 --> 00:22:33,292
of the spatial extent, right?

475
00:22:33,292 --> 00:22:35,250
AUDIENCE: So just in terms of input and output,

476
00:22:35,250 --> 00:22:39,127
not in terms of the throughput of the pretained models.

477
00:22:39,127 --> 00:22:40,710
SARA BEERY: Oh, you mean like actually

478
00:22:40,710 --> 00:22:42,850
changing the structure of the model itself?

479
00:22:42,850 --> 00:22:45,282
AUDIENCE: I guess like stacking on top of [INAUDIBLE].

480
00:22:45,282 --> 00:22:45,990
SARA BEERY: Yeah.

481
00:22:45,990 --> 00:22:48,490
I mean, so one of the things we're trying to get across here

482
00:22:48,490 --> 00:22:51,270
is that you can treat these things as modular building

483
00:22:51,270 --> 00:22:52,490
blocks, right?

484
00:22:52,490 --> 00:22:55,510
And so you can take a building block that someone else has

485
00:22:55,510 --> 00:22:58,250
built and then stick it in.

486
00:22:58,250 --> 00:22:59,830
And then you have to figure out what

487
00:22:59,830 --> 00:23:02,150
blocks you need to add to the system

488
00:23:02,150 --> 00:23:04,990
to maybe get that building block to be useful for you.

489
00:23:04,990 --> 00:23:08,430
But the point being that a really well-trained building

490
00:23:08,430 --> 00:23:11,310
block-- if that analogy still is making sense.

491
00:23:11,310 --> 00:23:13,850
A really well-trained building block is still useful,

492
00:23:13,850 --> 00:23:16,950
and it's maybe worth coming up with this modular structure that

493
00:23:16,950 --> 00:23:20,750
will make that work for your problem.

494
00:23:20,750 --> 00:23:22,965
But as soon as you start actually trying to mess

495
00:23:22,965 --> 00:23:24,590
with that building block, like changing

496
00:23:24,590 --> 00:23:26,330
the weights and the biases within that block,

497
00:23:26,330 --> 00:23:27,250
it gets more complicated.

498
00:23:27,250 --> 00:23:28,850
There are people who look at doing it,

499
00:23:28,850 --> 00:23:31,303
and actually, the entire area of model compression

500
00:23:31,303 --> 00:23:33,470
is kind of changing that structure by removing stuff

501
00:23:33,470 --> 00:23:34,490
to make it more efficient.

502
00:23:34,490 --> 00:23:36,365
And we'll talk a little bit about that later.

503
00:23:36,365 --> 00:23:39,150
In terms of adding more stuff, I don't know.

504
00:23:39,150 --> 00:23:41,290
I haven't seen a lot of work in that space,

505
00:23:41,290 --> 00:23:43,415
but I think as soon as you start adding more stuff,

506
00:23:43,415 --> 00:23:45,030
the value of the pretaining starts

507
00:23:45,030 --> 00:23:47,990
to go away, if that makes sense, because then you've

508
00:23:47,990 --> 00:23:51,910
changed the structure of the mathematical formulas.

509
00:23:51,910 --> 00:23:53,950
Cool.

510
00:23:53,950 --> 00:23:57,870
So essentially, we're just trying to figure out

511
00:23:57,870 --> 00:23:59,730
how to make things useful.

512
00:23:59,730 --> 00:24:05,270
And so in practice, we'll essentially just learn mappings

513
00:24:05,270 --> 00:24:09,495
for any new stuff we add to make this work for our data.

514
00:24:09,495 --> 00:24:11,870
And so those new things would need to be then initialized

515
00:24:11,870 --> 00:24:14,790
from scratch, but that's a lot less parameters

516
00:24:14,790 --> 00:24:17,790
that need to be learned from scratch than if you're going end

517
00:24:17,790 --> 00:24:20,950
to end from scratch, starting from that blank slate.

518
00:24:20,950 --> 00:24:23,730
And if you really don't have much data,

519
00:24:23,730 --> 00:24:26,650
you can also think about copying weights for that initialization.

520
00:24:26,650 --> 00:24:29,750
So for example, in that RGB-D case,

521
00:24:29,750 --> 00:24:31,590
sometimes what people will do is they

522
00:24:31,590 --> 00:24:33,950
will take the RGB channels that were learned

523
00:24:33,950 --> 00:24:35,550
from the original model, and then

524
00:24:35,550 --> 00:24:37,390
they'll staple on an additional channel,

525
00:24:37,390 --> 00:24:41,950
and they'll initialize it with, for example, just

526
00:24:41,950 --> 00:24:46,070
the blue channel parameters, with the idea that maybe there's

527
00:24:46,070 --> 00:24:49,350
some value in what's been learned for another channel that

528
00:24:49,350 --> 00:24:51,110
might still be transferable.

529
00:24:51,110 --> 00:24:56,690
Again, this is all kind of-- no one really knows.

530
00:24:56,690 --> 00:25:01,710
No has a perfect set of heuristics

531
00:25:01,710 --> 00:25:05,150
for, if this is your data, this is the best thing to do.

532
00:25:05,150 --> 00:25:06,550
And this is really where the idea

533
00:25:06,550 --> 00:25:08,590
of designing a benchmark for your data

534
00:25:08,590 --> 00:25:11,150
set where you understand how to do comparison

535
00:25:11,150 --> 00:25:13,130
across some of these different choices

536
00:25:13,130 --> 00:25:15,787
so you can try things and see how they're affecting

537
00:25:15,787 --> 00:25:17,870
performance of your model-- that's where it starts

538
00:25:17,870 --> 00:25:19,250
to become really important.

539
00:25:19,250 --> 00:25:23,520
Because most of the time, if it's some custom problem,

540
00:25:23,520 --> 00:25:25,020
some little dimension of the system

541
00:25:25,020 --> 00:25:27,160
will probably be somewhat customizable.

542
00:25:27,160 --> 00:25:31,820
And it's hard to say what's the best structure for a system

543
00:25:31,820 --> 00:25:35,700
without actually doing some testing.

544
00:25:35,700 --> 00:25:39,540
So one of the issues is that if you only

545
00:25:39,540 --> 00:25:44,420
have a little bit of data, and you let the entire model train,

546
00:25:44,420 --> 00:25:49,180
and you train it until that loss is all the way saturated--

547
00:25:49,180 --> 00:25:51,580
we don't have anything else we can learn--

548
00:25:51,580 --> 00:25:54,220
we run into that catastrophic forgetting problem

549
00:25:54,220 --> 00:25:56,580
again, that thing we talked about a bit

550
00:25:56,580 --> 00:25:58,582
when we were talking about sequences, where it's

551
00:25:58,582 --> 00:26:01,040
like, somehow, the thing we learned from the original data,

552
00:26:01,040 --> 00:26:02,480
we learned too long ago.

553
00:26:02,480 --> 00:26:04,900
The model's weights get changed too much,

554
00:26:04,900 --> 00:26:08,620
and you essentially lose capacity.

555
00:26:08,620 --> 00:26:12,660
So this definitely happens more with smaller models

556
00:26:12,660 --> 00:26:15,540
than big models, interestingly.

557
00:26:15,540 --> 00:26:18,500
But in order to avoid kind of overfitting

558
00:26:18,500 --> 00:26:21,660
on a really small amount of data for your specific task,

559
00:26:21,660 --> 00:26:24,500
there are a few different mechanisms

560
00:26:24,500 --> 00:26:28,060
that people use to try to strike that right balance, learning

561
00:26:28,060 --> 00:26:30,620
enough about the new task that you can specialize while not

562
00:26:30,620 --> 00:26:34,980
forgetting too much about this general useful representation.

563
00:26:34,980 --> 00:26:36,620
And so a couple of things.

564
00:26:36,620 --> 00:26:39,720
You can think about freezing most of the network.

565
00:26:39,720 --> 00:26:42,260
So basically saying, I just want to keep

566
00:26:42,260 --> 00:26:44,460
most of this model exactly the same,

567
00:26:44,460 --> 00:26:46,838
and only training, for example, the final layers or so,

568
00:26:46,838 --> 00:26:48,380
those initial things at the beginning

569
00:26:48,380 --> 00:26:50,700
that you need to have that are new.

570
00:26:50,700 --> 00:26:53,040
You can explicitly use really small learning rates.

571
00:26:53,040 --> 00:26:54,707
So basically, just not letting the model

572
00:26:54,707 --> 00:26:57,860
move too much in any direction at any training step.

573
00:26:57,860 --> 00:27:00,020
You can use what's called early stopping, which

574
00:27:00,020 --> 00:27:01,980
is-- essentially, you use a validation set,

575
00:27:01,980 --> 00:27:05,060
and you make a decision about which model weights

576
00:27:05,060 --> 00:27:07,980
to keep based on the best performance on your validation

577
00:27:07,980 --> 00:27:10,980
set with the idea that, essentially, you just haven't

578
00:27:10,980 --> 00:27:14,060
let the model train too much, so you stopped it

579
00:27:14,060 --> 00:27:17,780
before it can forget too much about the original pretext task.

580
00:27:17,780 --> 00:27:20,920
Or another mechanism-- and one that tends to work pretty well,

581
00:27:20,920 --> 00:27:23,503
though, of course, there's some complications in terms of data

582
00:27:23,503 --> 00:27:26,380
balance that you have to strike, so sampling effectively--

583
00:27:26,380 --> 00:27:30,140
is to just continue to train on the original data as well.

584
00:27:30,140 --> 00:27:33,460
So essentially, if you have access to the original training

585
00:27:33,460 --> 00:27:36,420
data, then as you're fine-tuning, continuing

586
00:27:36,420 --> 00:27:39,140
to add some of that pretext or some of that original training

587
00:27:39,140 --> 00:27:40,220
data is good.

588
00:27:40,220 --> 00:27:41,960
But of course, there's a complexity here,

589
00:27:41,960 --> 00:27:45,060
which is, all right, but if you have different input

590
00:27:45,060 --> 00:27:48,460
types and different goals, this becomes harder and harder to do.

591
00:27:48,460 --> 00:27:50,508
If you were doing contrastive pretaining,

592
00:27:50,508 --> 00:27:52,300
and now, you're trying to do classification

593
00:27:52,300 --> 00:27:54,480
on top of those representations, still

594
00:27:54,480 --> 00:27:56,980
continuing to have the contrastive pretaining is

595
00:27:56,980 --> 00:27:59,980
a little bit more complicated.

596
00:27:59,980 --> 00:28:03,572
But a lot of these choices, like these types of things,

597
00:28:03,572 --> 00:28:05,780
assume that you have some sort of good representative

598
00:28:05,780 --> 00:28:07,860
validation set, that your benchmark is well

599
00:28:07,860 --> 00:28:09,300
posed for your task.

600
00:28:09,300 --> 00:28:11,020
This might not always be possible,

601
00:28:11,020 --> 00:28:14,540
where you might see distribution shift going forward.

602
00:28:14,540 --> 00:28:16,740
And so it really does become quite

603
00:28:16,740 --> 00:28:20,980
important to think about measuring relative performance

604
00:28:20,980 --> 00:28:24,620
as close as possible to your representative problem.

605
00:28:24,620 --> 00:28:27,860
If you want to actually use the model on some data,

606
00:28:27,860 --> 00:28:31,420
defining measuring progress is just

607
00:28:31,420 --> 00:28:34,020
difficult and complicated and important.

608
00:28:34,020 --> 00:28:37,462
And this is-- now, I'm going to be on a soapbox a little bit.

609
00:28:37,462 --> 00:28:39,420
But one of the things that drives me absolutely

610
00:28:39,420 --> 00:28:41,060
crazy about modern machine learning

611
00:28:41,060 --> 00:28:43,340
is that we're obsessed with the idea of bolded numbers

612
00:28:43,340 --> 00:28:44,620
and tables.

613
00:28:44,620 --> 00:28:46,780
And so this kind of begs the question

614
00:28:46,780 --> 00:28:48,620
that you can represent everything

615
00:28:48,620 --> 00:28:52,220
about what a model does with one single number that

616
00:28:52,220 --> 00:28:55,540
captures every dimension of importance to that model.

617
00:28:55,540 --> 00:28:57,740
And I don't think that's true.

618
00:28:57,740 --> 00:28:59,240
I think we need nuance.

619
00:28:59,240 --> 00:29:01,820
And honestly, usually, people will--

620
00:29:01,820 --> 00:29:02,840
this is bad behavior.

621
00:29:02,840 --> 00:29:05,923
People will pick the metric that their model does best on.

622
00:29:05,923 --> 00:29:07,840
They're like, oh, this makes us look the best,

623
00:29:07,840 --> 00:29:10,080
so we're just going to show that one in our paper,

624
00:29:10,080 --> 00:29:11,830
and we're not going to talk about the fact

625
00:29:11,830 --> 00:29:14,740
that for some of these other metrics, we don't do as well.

626
00:29:14,740 --> 00:29:17,380
I really love it in a machine learning paper when people

627
00:29:17,380 --> 00:29:19,380
are honest about their flaws.

628
00:29:19,380 --> 00:29:21,680
Where you're like, hey, all right.

629
00:29:21,680 --> 00:29:24,320
We see some really great progress in these sections.

630
00:29:24,320 --> 00:29:26,580
And then you have a really nice limitations section

631
00:29:26,580 --> 00:29:30,100
that's realistic and captures the dimensions

632
00:29:30,100 --> 00:29:33,620
of where there still is space to make progress.

633
00:29:33,620 --> 00:29:35,120
Maybe it's just personal preference,

634
00:29:35,120 --> 00:29:36,920
but I'm a little bit allergic to salesmanship,

635
00:29:36,920 --> 00:29:38,545
so I don't like it when I read a paper,

636
00:29:38,545 --> 00:29:41,540
and I'm like, I don't know, man.

637
00:29:41,540 --> 00:29:43,935
I think you're sweeping some stuff under the rug.

638
00:29:43,935 --> 00:29:46,560
Essentially, you do not want me to be your reviewer number two.

639
00:29:50,640 --> 00:29:51,140
Yeah.

640
00:29:51,140 --> 00:29:53,060
OK.

641
00:29:53,060 --> 00:29:55,560
So we'd like to avoid forgetting the general representation,

642
00:29:55,560 --> 00:29:57,020
and here's some engineering hacks

643
00:29:57,020 --> 00:29:59,300
that you can take if you want to try and do that.

644
00:29:59,300 --> 00:30:02,660
But then there's also this other kind of area of research

645
00:30:02,660 --> 00:30:05,480
that's specifically-- it's called domain adaptation.

646
00:30:05,480 --> 00:30:08,300
And the idea here is that maybe you have some source domain,

647
00:30:08,300 --> 00:30:10,340
and you have a target domain, and then

648
00:30:10,340 --> 00:30:13,430
you want to learn to do well on your target domain,

649
00:30:13,430 --> 00:30:15,690
but you also want to essentially make sure

650
00:30:15,690 --> 00:30:19,290
that the representation spaces of the source and target domain

651
00:30:19,290 --> 00:30:20,970
are well aligned.

652
00:30:20,970 --> 00:30:22,810
And this shows up a lot when you have

653
00:30:22,810 --> 00:30:24,810
these types of distribution shift problems

654
00:30:24,810 --> 00:30:26,490
that we talked about before.

655
00:30:26,490 --> 00:30:29,832
But it also can be used for different types of fine-tuning.

656
00:30:29,832 --> 00:30:31,290
Essentially, one of the motivations

657
00:30:31,290 --> 00:30:35,090
here is that maybe if you force these feature spaces to be

658
00:30:35,090 --> 00:30:42,330
well aligned generally, that you can learn usefully how to map--

659
00:30:42,330 --> 00:30:44,470
learn that mapping with much less data.

660
00:30:44,470 --> 00:30:47,490
And one way that people do this is they actually--

661
00:30:47,490 --> 00:30:52,210
they learn how to get the model from the source domain

662
00:30:52,210 --> 00:30:54,730
to the outputs, and then they try

663
00:30:54,730 --> 00:30:57,890
to learn a projection or a mapping from the target domain

664
00:30:57,890 --> 00:30:58,950
to the source domain.

665
00:30:58,950 --> 00:31:01,850
So you basically are learning how to map into the domain space

666
00:31:01,850 --> 00:31:05,370
where you had a lot of data, and then you can use the same model

667
00:31:05,370 --> 00:31:07,513
to actually do your prediction.

668
00:31:07,513 --> 00:31:08,930
And there's lots of different ways

669
00:31:08,930 --> 00:31:11,090
to try to force these representation spaces to be

670
00:31:11,090 --> 00:31:15,250
aligned, but one kind of large set of them

671
00:31:15,250 --> 00:31:17,170
takes an adversarial approach.

672
00:31:17,170 --> 00:31:21,610
So here, you have both source and target data during training.

673
00:31:21,610 --> 00:31:26,170
And while you're training for every input,

674
00:31:26,170 --> 00:31:29,090
you want to predict the category you care about,

675
00:31:29,090 --> 00:31:31,370
and you also want to predict the domain

676
00:31:31,370 --> 00:31:34,330
that it came from, something about the distribution.

677
00:31:34,330 --> 00:31:38,530
So a classic example of a data set in this domain adaptation

678
00:31:38,530 --> 00:31:44,090
space is the data set of different types

679
00:31:44,090 --> 00:31:46,330
of technological hardware.

680
00:31:46,330 --> 00:31:48,990
But one data set is from Amazon, and one is from eBay.

681
00:31:48,990 --> 00:31:51,330
So you can think that, OK, the way that people

682
00:31:51,330 --> 00:31:53,763
take pictures for Amazon are very nice and clean,

683
00:31:53,763 --> 00:31:55,930
and then the way people are taking pictures for eBay

684
00:31:55,930 --> 00:31:58,267
is maybe like on their messy desk.

685
00:31:58,267 --> 00:31:59,850
And so there, what you're trying to do

686
00:31:59,850 --> 00:32:02,390
is you're trying to predict, what kind of computer is it?

687
00:32:02,390 --> 00:32:03,878
And is it from Amazon or eBay?

688
00:32:03,878 --> 00:32:05,670
And then this is where it gets adversarial.

689
00:32:05,670 --> 00:32:08,570
What they do is they flip the gradient

690
00:32:08,570 --> 00:32:10,890
from the domain predictor.

691
00:32:10,890 --> 00:32:12,450
So you're essentially trying to train

692
00:32:12,450 --> 00:32:14,770
the model to be as good as possible

693
00:32:14,770 --> 00:32:18,530
at predicting the category, while being as bad as possible

694
00:32:18,530 --> 00:32:21,370
at predicting the domain, which tries to force, essentially,

695
00:32:21,370 --> 00:32:25,515
the representations across the two domains to align.

696
00:32:25,515 --> 00:32:26,890
And then the interesting thing is

697
00:32:26,890 --> 00:32:30,250
you can extend this to where you might not actually

698
00:32:30,250 --> 00:32:35,070
have category data for the source and the target.

699
00:32:35,070 --> 00:32:38,130
So maybe you had a bunch of category data for the source,

700
00:32:38,130 --> 00:32:40,050
and now, all you have for the target domain

701
00:32:40,050 --> 00:32:42,250
is just the knowledge of what domain it came from.

702
00:32:42,250 --> 00:32:44,490
You can think about trying to still

703
00:32:44,490 --> 00:32:47,130
keep really good at predicting the category,

704
00:32:47,130 --> 00:32:50,050
but be really bad at predicting which domain it's from.

705
00:32:50,050 --> 00:32:52,450
And the hope is that there, even if you only

706
00:32:52,450 --> 00:32:55,710
have a very limited amount of labeled data in your target,

707
00:32:55,710 --> 00:32:57,690
or even sometimes none, you might still

708
00:32:57,690 --> 00:32:59,130
be able to learn something useful

709
00:32:59,130 --> 00:33:01,490
that helps you find the right categories in that target

710
00:33:01,490 --> 00:33:03,610
domain.

711
00:33:03,610 --> 00:33:05,570
And then this is kind of expanded

712
00:33:05,570 --> 00:33:08,410
into this whole area of unsupervised domain adaptation,

713
00:33:08,410 --> 00:33:12,010
where here the idea is you might have data, but no labels

714
00:33:12,010 --> 00:33:13,970
from the target domain.

715
00:33:13,970 --> 00:33:16,250
So now, it's like you want to make a model that's

716
00:33:16,250 --> 00:33:19,210
as good as possible on some set of data,

717
00:33:19,210 --> 00:33:22,370
but you don't necessarily have any training labels.

718
00:33:22,370 --> 00:33:23,750
Now, why would we have that?

719
00:33:23,750 --> 00:33:28,050
So here's an example, actually from our own research,

720
00:33:28,050 --> 00:33:29,948
where we have a bunch of labeled source data.

721
00:33:29,948 --> 00:33:31,490
In this case, the labeled source data

722
00:33:31,490 --> 00:33:36,178
is sonar images or sonar video from rivers with fish.

723
00:33:36,178 --> 00:33:37,970
And we're trying to count all the fish that

724
00:33:37,970 --> 00:33:40,512
are swimming upstream so we can make good sustainable fishing

725
00:33:40,512 --> 00:33:41,650
policies.

726
00:33:41,650 --> 00:33:46,490
And we have some rivers where we have a bunch of labeled data,

727
00:33:46,490 --> 00:33:49,092
but we want this to work on any river.

728
00:33:49,092 --> 00:33:51,050
Or if something changes about the river or they

729
00:33:51,050 --> 00:33:52,800
move the sensor, we want it to still work,

730
00:33:52,800 --> 00:33:54,930
even though there's that distribution shift.

731
00:33:54,930 --> 00:33:56,650
And so here, what we're trying to do

732
00:33:56,650 --> 00:34:00,190
is learn from labeled source data and unlabeled target data

733
00:34:00,190 --> 00:34:02,890
how get a model to work better on the target.

734
00:34:02,890 --> 00:34:04,670
So how have people done this in the past?

735
00:34:04,670 --> 00:34:08,679
Well, so the very, very simple version of this would be,

736
00:34:08,679 --> 00:34:10,719
you take your target data set, you run it

737
00:34:10,719 --> 00:34:13,199
through a model that's been pretained on source,

738
00:34:13,199 --> 00:34:15,020
and then you maybe just calibrate it.

739
00:34:15,020 --> 00:34:16,770
You pick some threshold where you say, OK.

740
00:34:16,770 --> 00:34:20,679
Any prediction over this, we're going to treat as ground truth.

741
00:34:20,679 --> 00:34:25,040
So that's one way to transfer knowledge.

742
00:34:25,040 --> 00:34:27,880
You just use a model from a different domain

743
00:34:27,880 --> 00:34:29,000
and hope it works.

744
00:34:29,000 --> 00:34:31,920
It turns out it doesn't usually work very well.

745
00:34:31,920 --> 00:34:34,639
So this kind of motivated this idea-- and this

746
00:34:34,639 --> 00:34:37,239
is a mechanism to do distillation, which we'll talk

747
00:34:37,239 --> 00:34:39,480
about a little bit more later.

748
00:34:39,480 --> 00:34:43,880
But what if, instead, we make a new model, that we're

749
00:34:43,880 --> 00:34:45,639
going to call a student model, and we're

750
00:34:45,639 --> 00:34:47,800
going to use those predictions on the target

751
00:34:47,800 --> 00:34:50,315
data as weak supervision?

752
00:34:50,315 --> 00:34:52,440
So we'll use the predictions that our model trained

753
00:34:52,440 --> 00:34:55,120
on the source data gives, and then we'll

754
00:34:55,120 --> 00:34:59,120
try to train a model to predict the same thing.

755
00:34:59,120 --> 00:35:01,280
And the idea here is that maybe your student model

756
00:35:01,280 --> 00:35:03,680
is able to learned something useful

757
00:35:03,680 --> 00:35:07,628
that is better than just taking those raw predictions.

758
00:35:07,628 --> 00:35:08,420
And this does work.

759
00:35:08,420 --> 00:35:11,360
You get small gains, sometimes, over just the original source

760
00:35:11,360 --> 00:35:12,440
predictions.

761
00:35:12,440 --> 00:35:14,360
But this threshold is really, really

762
00:35:14,360 --> 00:35:17,400
finicky and hard to calibrate, right?

763
00:35:17,400 --> 00:35:21,280
So people started adding in other bells and whistles.

764
00:35:21,280 --> 00:35:24,860
One thing is you use EMA, an Exponential Moving Average,

765
00:35:24,860 --> 00:35:27,720
to try to regularize the way that the weights change

766
00:35:27,720 --> 00:35:29,640
within the student and teacher model,

767
00:35:29,640 --> 00:35:34,080
so you let the teacher model itself also update slower.

768
00:35:34,080 --> 00:35:37,240
And then, finally, you can also introduce additional training

769
00:35:37,240 --> 00:35:38,600
on the source data.

770
00:35:38,600 --> 00:35:41,280
So there, maybe you're letting your student model

771
00:35:41,280 --> 00:35:44,460
learn from the source data and from the target data.

772
00:35:44,460 --> 00:35:46,740
And then you can add in a bunch of fancy stuff,

773
00:35:46,740 --> 00:35:49,840
like instead of explicitly taking

774
00:35:49,840 --> 00:35:51,300
the predictions with a threshold,

775
00:35:51,300 --> 00:35:52,740
you can use a softmax.

776
00:35:52,740 --> 00:35:55,040
You can use a distillation loss so that you're

777
00:35:55,040 --> 00:35:57,340
learning to, instead of match the prediction,

778
00:35:57,340 --> 00:36:00,080
match the distribution over the outputs.

779
00:36:00,080 --> 00:36:02,400
And you can try to use some explicit feature

780
00:36:02,400 --> 00:36:04,180
alignment between source and target,

781
00:36:04,180 --> 00:36:06,013
so that's like basically trying to make sure

782
00:36:06,013 --> 00:36:08,720
that the features are overlapping as much as possible.

783
00:36:08,720 --> 00:36:10,720
Lots of fancy tricks here.

784
00:36:10,720 --> 00:36:13,400
Kind of complicated.

785
00:36:13,400 --> 00:36:15,760
We essentially wanted to point out

786
00:36:15,760 --> 00:36:18,520
that these components are essentially

787
00:36:18,520 --> 00:36:20,040
the main components that people use

788
00:36:20,040 --> 00:36:25,720
to do this type of distribution, unsupervised domain adaptation.

789
00:36:25,720 --> 00:36:29,120
And the point here is that basically every modern domain

790
00:36:29,120 --> 00:36:32,360
adaptation architecture is some combination of these components.

791
00:36:32,360 --> 00:36:36,840
And so if you can centralize and unify the ways

792
00:36:36,840 --> 00:36:40,520
that people are actually doing these experiments, what we found

793
00:36:40,520 --> 00:36:43,080
is that you can often be more optimal

794
00:36:43,080 --> 00:36:45,720
than any previous approach.

795
00:36:45,720 --> 00:36:50,040
And so these methods, many of them

796
00:36:50,040 --> 00:36:52,220
can be quite effective in certain scenarios.

797
00:36:52,220 --> 00:36:55,980
Of course, certain scenarios is a big interesting thing there.

798
00:36:55,980 --> 00:36:58,440
They're almost never as effective as having labeled

799
00:36:58,440 --> 00:36:59,860
data for your source domain.

800
00:36:59,860 --> 00:37:02,440
So if you just have labels, that's better.

801
00:37:02,440 --> 00:37:04,080
But if you don't have labels, you

802
00:37:04,080 --> 00:37:06,400
can actually start to get pretty close to performance

803
00:37:06,400 --> 00:37:10,040
if you do have labeled data if you

804
00:37:10,040 --> 00:37:12,460
build these things carefully.

805
00:37:12,460 --> 00:37:15,020
And some intuition as to why that way might be

806
00:37:15,020 --> 00:37:17,263
is that, essentially, the model is

807
00:37:17,263 --> 00:37:19,680
able to learn something about the statistics of the target

808
00:37:19,680 --> 00:37:21,920
domain that's useful.

809
00:37:21,920 --> 00:37:24,200
You're shifting the weights towards being

810
00:37:24,200 --> 00:37:26,520
representative on those new statistics.

811
00:37:26,520 --> 00:37:27,520
Yeah?

812
00:37:27,520 --> 00:37:31,160
AUDIENCE: How can you measure how the performance of this

813
00:37:31,160 --> 00:37:32,360
when deployed--

814
00:37:32,360 --> 00:37:35,200
if you deploy it in a field that you actually

815
00:37:35,200 --> 00:37:38,000
don't have the labels of anything,

816
00:37:38,000 --> 00:37:39,872
how do you know your models did well?

817
00:37:39,872 --> 00:37:40,580
SARA BEERY: Yeah.

818
00:37:40,580 --> 00:37:41,883
This is a really great point.

819
00:37:41,883 --> 00:37:43,800
I think it's actually the elephant in the room

820
00:37:43,800 --> 00:37:46,200
for most of the domain adaptation literature

821
00:37:46,200 --> 00:37:48,760
is that they almost always are demonstrating performance

822
00:37:48,760 --> 00:37:51,080
in their bold number in their table

823
00:37:51,080 --> 00:37:54,640
on labeled validation data for the target domain.

824
00:37:54,640 --> 00:37:56,340
But this kind of begs the question,

825
00:37:56,340 --> 00:37:59,160
if you have to label data to do model selection

826
00:37:59,160 --> 00:38:01,140
and determine what mechanism works best,

827
00:38:01,140 --> 00:38:03,700
why don't you just use that labeled data for training?

828
00:38:06,880 --> 00:38:07,380
Yeah.

829
00:38:07,380 --> 00:38:11,420
I mean, I think it's kind of one of these interesting questions.

830
00:38:11,420 --> 00:38:13,520
And also, if you did have some training data,

831
00:38:13,520 --> 00:38:15,960
how does that kind of fit into this system?

832
00:38:15,960 --> 00:38:20,120
But yeah, I do think that in the literature in this space,

833
00:38:20,120 --> 00:38:23,945
one of the big missing pieces is that you still

834
00:38:23,945 --> 00:38:26,320
aren't very good at doing this if you don't have anything

835
00:38:26,320 --> 00:38:32,320
to select the best model or calibrate it in any way at all.

836
00:38:32,320 --> 00:38:32,840
Cool.

837
00:38:32,840 --> 00:38:33,640
Yeah?

838
00:38:33,640 --> 00:38:35,600
AUDIENCE: I guess following up on that, I mean,

839
00:38:35,600 --> 00:38:39,880
isn't the point to reduce costs and time because you

840
00:38:39,880 --> 00:38:42,760
can use bigger data sets that are available?

841
00:38:42,760 --> 00:38:47,020
SARA BEERY: So reducing costs and time are is valuable here.

842
00:38:47,020 --> 00:38:49,040
So maybe one dimension is you're trying

843
00:38:49,040 --> 00:38:51,920
to reduce the amount of human input

844
00:38:51,920 --> 00:38:54,180
you need to get a model to work well.

845
00:38:54,180 --> 00:38:56,320
But I think probably a better motivated scenario

846
00:38:56,320 --> 00:38:59,150
for a lot of these is places where we do not

847
00:38:59,150 --> 00:39:02,190
have the right bandwidth to be able to actually get

848
00:39:02,190 --> 00:39:04,590
the data to where a human could look at it easily.

849
00:39:04,590 --> 00:39:06,950
So we use these domain adaptation models

850
00:39:06,950 --> 00:39:10,150
on these rivers that are in the remote Arctic,

851
00:39:10,150 --> 00:39:14,090
where the way for a human to get there is fly into Anchorage,

852
00:39:14,090 --> 00:39:17,110
take a seaplane, hike.

853
00:39:17,110 --> 00:39:20,408
It's just nontrivial to actually get to the sensor,

854
00:39:20,408 --> 00:39:22,950
and we don't have the kind of satellite connectivity or power

855
00:39:22,950 --> 00:39:26,730
that we would need to get the data to the cloud.

856
00:39:26,730 --> 00:39:29,270
So if you have these strong resource constraints

857
00:39:29,270 --> 00:39:31,790
on your system where you can't necessarily just have

858
00:39:31,790 --> 00:39:33,790
a human easily label data, I think

859
00:39:33,790 --> 00:39:36,510
that's where these types of models tend to make more sense.

860
00:39:36,510 --> 00:39:38,750
Similarly, if you're-- I don't know--

861
00:39:38,750 --> 00:39:43,790
doing navigation on the moon or deep sea, sometimes,

862
00:39:43,790 --> 00:39:46,270
we just need models that hopefully can adapt

863
00:39:46,270 --> 00:39:47,570
to a distribution.

864
00:39:47,570 --> 00:39:49,070
But the problem is if you don't have

865
00:39:49,070 --> 00:39:51,670
any kind of quality control going in,

866
00:39:51,670 --> 00:39:55,670
anything like this that's kind of learning from data where we

867
00:39:55,670 --> 00:39:58,150
don't have any of true labels, has

868
00:39:58,150 --> 00:40:00,070
the potential, from a reinforcement learning

869
00:40:00,070 --> 00:40:03,715
perspective, to veer off in some catastrophic way.

870
00:40:03,715 --> 00:40:05,590
And so you can fall into these kind of cycles

871
00:40:05,590 --> 00:40:07,840
where the model kind of gets worse and worse and worse

872
00:40:07,840 --> 00:40:09,612
and worse.

873
00:40:09,612 --> 00:40:12,070
And then the other question is, how much source data do you

874
00:40:12,070 --> 00:40:13,370
still have to have access to?

875
00:40:13,370 --> 00:40:17,430
So I think essentially, there's a lot of engineering components

876
00:40:17,430 --> 00:40:20,230
to why you would want to do this and where it's actually

877
00:40:20,230 --> 00:40:24,330
effective and what the value add is.

878
00:40:24,330 --> 00:40:24,830
Yeah.

879
00:40:27,890 --> 00:40:32,210
But I think there's also an interesting dimension here,

880
00:40:32,210 --> 00:40:36,310
which is these types of domain adaptation approaches also

881
00:40:36,310 --> 00:40:39,870
really benefit from the right starting point.

882
00:40:39,870 --> 00:40:42,270
So you can almost think of this as a different mechanism

883
00:40:42,270 --> 00:40:45,150
for fine-tuning, but the performance of these

884
00:40:45,150 --> 00:40:47,670
is very, very heavily influenced by the performance

885
00:40:47,670 --> 00:40:49,270
of the starting point model.

886
00:40:49,270 --> 00:40:50,950
So it's almost complementary in terms

887
00:40:50,950 --> 00:40:54,270
of trying to build the most generalizable model, the most

888
00:40:54,270 --> 00:40:56,390
useful general representation.

889
00:40:56,390 --> 00:40:58,270
And then this is maybe a mechanism

890
00:40:58,270 --> 00:41:00,830
to fine-tune if you have a lot of data that doesn't have

891
00:41:00,830 --> 00:41:03,710
labels, instead of maybe a little bit of data

892
00:41:03,710 --> 00:41:06,110
that does have labels.

893
00:41:06,110 --> 00:41:07,430
Cool.

894
00:41:07,430 --> 00:41:10,790
So next, I'm going to talk a little bit about how

895
00:41:10,790 --> 00:41:13,310
we might try to transfer information or knowledge

896
00:41:13,310 --> 00:41:16,830
about the outputs of a model.

897
00:41:16,830 --> 00:41:20,550
So if you recall before, we talked

898
00:41:20,550 --> 00:41:22,670
about how you train a classifier with something

899
00:41:22,670 --> 00:41:24,910
like cross entropy.

900
00:41:24,910 --> 00:41:27,910
So here, we have some model.

901
00:41:27,910 --> 00:41:30,130
We have an input, x.

902
00:41:30,130 --> 00:41:32,630
We have some output, y, which is the ground

903
00:41:32,630 --> 00:41:34,750
truth for that model-- or y is the output

904
00:41:34,750 --> 00:41:36,190
prediction for that model.

905
00:41:36,190 --> 00:41:38,330
So maybe there's a bunch of different categories,

906
00:41:38,330 --> 00:41:44,310
and the one that has the largest prediction score or logit score

907
00:41:44,310 --> 00:41:45,560
is cat.

908
00:41:45,560 --> 00:41:47,310
And maybe, it's taken a softmax, actually,

909
00:41:47,310 --> 00:41:49,870
because it's going from 0 to 1, so this is maybe

910
00:41:49,870 --> 00:41:51,350
our prediction score.

911
00:41:51,350 --> 00:41:52,910
And then the ground truth is going

912
00:41:52,910 --> 00:41:58,390
to be some one hot encoded label, where we say, all right.

913
00:41:58,390 --> 00:42:01,550
The true label for this input, x, is cat.

914
00:42:01,550 --> 00:42:04,070
And then we use cross entropy loss

915
00:42:04,070 --> 00:42:08,110
that basically tries to get our model

916
00:42:08,110 --> 00:42:10,390
to predict a distribution that's as close as

917
00:42:10,390 --> 00:42:13,270
possible to this ground truth distribution, which is just 1

918
00:42:13,270 --> 00:42:17,150
for cat and 0 for everything else.

919
00:42:17,150 --> 00:42:19,190
So you could argue that this is also kind

920
00:42:19,190 --> 00:42:21,870
of a teacher and a student.

921
00:42:21,870 --> 00:42:27,030
So here, the teacher is the difference between the ground

922
00:42:27,030 --> 00:42:31,070
truth and our prediction, and the student is kind of the way

923
00:42:31,070 --> 00:42:33,350
that we're capturing that distance.

924
00:42:33,350 --> 00:42:37,550
And so the idea of knowledge distillation is essentially,

925
00:42:37,550 --> 00:42:41,630
now, we have some big pretained model.

926
00:42:41,630 --> 00:42:43,770
We're going to assume that model is fixed.

927
00:42:43,770 --> 00:42:47,510
So we have this little lock on here, so it's a locked model.

928
00:42:47,510 --> 00:42:53,510
And then we want to learn some student where-- so now, we

929
00:42:53,510 --> 00:42:57,710
go from x to y teacher, and we have a model that

930
00:42:57,710 --> 00:43:00,150
goes from x to y student.

931
00:43:00,150 --> 00:43:04,310
And then the way that we try to get this model to distill

932
00:43:04,310 --> 00:43:09,190
is we want the difference or the distance between the teacher

933
00:43:09,190 --> 00:43:12,230
model's predictions and the student's model's predictions

934
00:43:12,230 --> 00:43:14,430
to be as close as possible.

935
00:43:14,430 --> 00:43:18,070
So now, maybe your teacher model has this distribution,

936
00:43:18,070 --> 00:43:20,170
and maybe it's predicting that cat is quite high,

937
00:43:20,170 --> 00:43:22,253
but it's also predicting that tiger is quite high.

938
00:43:22,253 --> 00:43:26,790
So now, when we're actually training our student model,

939
00:43:26,790 --> 00:43:30,590
we want it to similarly match that distribution.

940
00:43:30,590 --> 00:43:34,390
Now, does anyone have any intuition as to why this might

941
00:43:34,390 --> 00:43:38,590
be useful, why it might be useful to train a model

942
00:43:38,590 --> 00:43:40,910
to match a distribution of predictions

943
00:43:40,910 --> 00:43:43,470
as opposed to just a ground truth?

944
00:43:43,470 --> 00:43:44,350
Yeah.

945
00:43:44,350 --> 00:43:47,362
AUDIENCE: Maybe the new model is smaller and quicker.

946
00:43:47,362 --> 00:43:48,070
SARA BEERY: Yeah.

947
00:43:48,070 --> 00:43:50,500
So maybe it's a way to get a new model that's

948
00:43:50,500 --> 00:43:52,720
smaller, can be more computationally efficient,

949
00:43:52,720 --> 00:43:55,860
and can learn the same thing.

950
00:43:55,860 --> 00:43:58,700
Anyone have any intuition about why actually it

951
00:43:58,700 --> 00:44:04,380
might be valuable to match the distribution of the outputs?

952
00:44:04,380 --> 00:44:05,360
Maybe someone new.

953
00:44:05,360 --> 00:44:06,900
Yeah.

954
00:44:06,900 --> 00:44:09,100
AUDIENCE: If there's a cat and a tiger [INAUDIBLE]

955
00:44:09,100 --> 00:44:10,440
example are kind of similar.

956
00:44:10,440 --> 00:44:12,190
SARA BEERY: Yeah, exactly.

957
00:44:12,190 --> 00:44:13,440
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] animals.

958
00:44:13,440 --> 00:44:16,540
Maybe a dog's more similar than the monkey.

959
00:44:16,540 --> 00:44:18,400
[INAUDIBLE] a little bit to the dog.

960
00:44:18,400 --> 00:44:20,842
It'll give it some more information.

961
00:44:20,842 --> 00:44:22,300
SARA BEERY: Yeah, so that's exactly

962
00:44:22,300 --> 00:44:23,508
the answer I was looking for.

963
00:44:23,508 --> 00:44:25,900
It's giving the model some information

964
00:44:25,900 --> 00:44:28,260
about the similarity.

965
00:44:28,260 --> 00:44:30,900
So here, cats and tigers are maybe similar,

966
00:44:30,900 --> 00:44:33,740
but it's not just the similarity of the category set,

967
00:44:33,740 --> 00:44:39,920
it's the similarity of what's recognizable in a given input.

968
00:44:39,920 --> 00:44:44,420
So if you have an image of a cat, but it's an orange cat,

969
00:44:44,420 --> 00:44:47,060
and it's in tall grass, you could

970
00:44:47,060 --> 00:44:50,180
see how a model would say, hey, that's pretty

971
00:44:50,180 --> 00:44:53,140
similar to a lot of the data I've seen of tigers.

972
00:44:53,140 --> 00:44:55,780
And so now, what you're teaching the model is not just,

973
00:44:55,780 --> 00:44:57,880
this is a cat, you're teaching it,

974
00:44:57,880 --> 00:45:01,020
this is a cat that looks a little bit like a tiger.

975
00:45:01,020 --> 00:45:03,300
And that might actually be useful context

976
00:45:03,300 --> 00:45:06,660
for a model in terms of learning more than just

977
00:45:06,660 --> 00:45:10,980
a set of separable categories.

978
00:45:10,980 --> 00:45:16,500
And so there's another kind of interesting point

979
00:45:16,500 --> 00:45:19,220
here that I think is important to raise that's not just about

980
00:45:19,220 --> 00:45:25,220
efficiency but is about the modern era of many closed source

981
00:45:25,220 --> 00:45:26,460
models.

982
00:45:26,460 --> 00:45:30,260
So if you have access to the outputs of a model,

983
00:45:30,260 --> 00:45:33,180
its predictive scores, but not the model

984
00:45:33,180 --> 00:45:37,020
itself, you could arguably learn a model

985
00:45:37,020 --> 00:45:40,527
that can match the predictive scores of a given output.

986
00:45:40,527 --> 00:45:42,860
And this is maybe one of the reasons that a lot of those

987
00:45:42,860 --> 00:45:44,460
closed source models don't actually

988
00:45:44,460 --> 00:45:49,860
give you output predictions over the whole category set.

989
00:45:49,860 --> 00:45:50,620
Cool.

990
00:45:50,620 --> 00:45:53,020
So now, you can essentially think

991
00:45:53,020 --> 00:45:55,080
about doing standard cross entropy,

992
00:45:55,080 --> 00:45:58,460
but instead of cross entropy being to that binary category,

993
00:45:58,460 --> 00:46:02,580
it's just between the teacher and the student outputs.

994
00:46:02,580 --> 00:46:05,840
And so exactly like what you said before--

995
00:46:05,840 --> 00:46:07,300
you guys are great here--

996
00:46:07,300 --> 00:46:08,780
the label you get for every input

997
00:46:08,780 --> 00:46:11,260
might be more informative than just the ground truth

998
00:46:11,260 --> 00:46:14,420
class, and so this soft target is telling you

999
00:46:14,420 --> 00:46:19,140
something about the similarity and what things look like.

1000
00:46:19,140 --> 00:46:20,182
So this is a fun example.

1001
00:46:20,182 --> 00:46:22,348
It's not just saying this is a dog, but it's saying,

1002
00:46:22,348 --> 00:46:24,460
this is a dog that looks a little bit like a cat,

1003
00:46:24,460 --> 00:46:26,720
and it looks very different from a bear.

1004
00:46:26,720 --> 00:46:29,580
And so maybe that's telling you oh, it's a small dog.

1005
00:46:29,580 --> 00:46:33,940
It's-- I don't know-- one of those little yappy dogs.

1006
00:46:33,940 --> 00:46:36,640
So this original introductory paper,

1007
00:46:36,640 --> 00:46:39,320
which is one of Jeff Hinton's, shows

1008
00:46:39,320 --> 00:46:43,220
that you might actually get lower training accuracy when

1009
00:46:43,220 --> 00:46:45,020
training this way, but you end up

1010
00:46:45,020 --> 00:46:50,660
getting higher test accuracy on this speech recognition task.

1011
00:46:50,660 --> 00:46:53,740
And so the other interesting thing

1012
00:46:53,740 --> 00:46:56,500
is if these soft targets have really high entropy,

1013
00:46:56,500 --> 00:46:58,860
they're giving a lot more information per training case

1014
00:46:58,860 --> 00:46:59,960
than hard targets.

1015
00:46:59,960 --> 00:47:02,192
And they have a lot less variance in the gradient

1016
00:47:02,192 --> 00:47:03,900
between training cases-- is another thing

1017
00:47:03,900 --> 00:47:05,500
that they showed in this paper.

1018
00:47:05,500 --> 00:47:08,300
And so a small model can often be

1019
00:47:08,300 --> 00:47:12,240
trained on a lot less data than the original model

1020
00:47:12,240 --> 00:47:14,180
while using a much higher learning rate

1021
00:47:14,180 --> 00:47:17,140
when you're doing this type of distillation.

1022
00:47:17,140 --> 00:47:21,840
But it's not just something you can use to make a smaller model.

1023
00:47:21,840 --> 00:47:24,140
You can also think about learning from a distillation

1024
00:47:24,140 --> 00:47:26,620
perspective across modalities.

1025
00:47:26,620 --> 00:47:29,660
So here, maybe you have a teacher

1026
00:47:29,660 --> 00:47:34,960
that learns to predict categories from an RGB image,

1027
00:47:34,960 --> 00:47:37,980
and then you have a student that only has access to depth.

1028
00:47:37,980 --> 00:47:41,520
So this might be useful if you have labeled data for RGB,

1029
00:47:41,520 --> 00:47:45,490
but you don't necessarily have labeled data for depth.

1030
00:47:45,490 --> 00:47:50,250
And so another example of this cross-modal distillation

1031
00:47:50,250 --> 00:47:53,530
is this model called SoundNet where

1032
00:47:53,530 --> 00:47:59,090
they have waveforms and unlabeled video,

1033
00:47:59,090 --> 00:48:02,370
and then they essentially are doing object distributions

1034
00:48:02,370 --> 00:48:05,250
and scene distributions, and then they're

1035
00:48:05,250 --> 00:48:08,370
learning distillation across both.

1036
00:48:08,370 --> 00:48:10,290
There's another interesting version

1037
00:48:10,290 --> 00:48:13,610
of distillation from a computational complexity

1038
00:48:13,610 --> 00:48:16,570
perspective where you can actually distill

1039
00:48:16,570 --> 00:48:18,150
the results of an ensemble.

1040
00:48:18,150 --> 00:48:23,530
So often, we find that, even in the modern era,

1041
00:48:23,530 --> 00:48:26,050
ensembles over lots of models are often

1042
00:48:26,050 --> 00:48:29,970
more predictive than any single model.

1043
00:48:29,970 --> 00:48:32,650
One of the arguments for this is kind of a classical one, which

1044
00:48:32,650 --> 00:48:37,370
is that every model is going to hopefully make

1045
00:48:37,370 --> 00:48:40,050
the same correct answers more often than they

1046
00:48:40,050 --> 00:48:43,250
make the same mistakes.

1047
00:48:43,250 --> 00:48:45,210
And so here, what you can think about

1048
00:48:45,210 --> 00:48:47,090
is maybe you have an ensemble that's really

1049
00:48:47,090 --> 00:48:50,042
great ensemble, an ensemble of three different models,

1050
00:48:50,042 --> 00:48:51,750
but they're all kind of expensive to run,

1051
00:48:51,750 --> 00:48:53,450
and you don't really want to be running three models all

1052
00:48:53,450 --> 00:48:54,290
the time.

1053
00:48:54,290 --> 00:48:57,370
So you can think about explicitly taking

1054
00:48:57,370 --> 00:48:59,370
that ensemble of model outputs and then

1055
00:48:59,370 --> 00:49:03,090
trying to distill a model that matches the distribution

1056
00:49:03,090 --> 00:49:04,530
of your ensemble.

1057
00:49:04,530 --> 00:49:06,450
And again, each model is imperfect

1058
00:49:06,450 --> 00:49:08,050
in different random ways.

1059
00:49:08,050 --> 00:49:10,300
Errors cancel each other out, and the truth is shared.

1060
00:49:10,300 --> 00:49:11,717
Of course, this isn't always true,

1061
00:49:11,717 --> 00:49:13,130
because sometimes, we have biases

1062
00:49:13,130 --> 00:49:15,213
in models that are learned from our data that will

1063
00:49:15,213 --> 00:49:16,810
be consistent for every model.

1064
00:49:16,810 --> 00:49:18,810
So the thing you're not going to get out of this

1065
00:49:18,810 --> 00:49:21,770
is something that's really good at the types of corner cases

1066
00:49:21,770 --> 00:49:26,170
that every single model in your ensemble gets incorrect.

1067
00:49:26,170 --> 00:49:30,890
But it can be much more efficient.

1068
00:49:30,890 --> 00:49:32,250
Cool.

1069
00:49:32,250 --> 00:49:35,530
So what if you don't just want to distill the outputs,

1070
00:49:35,530 --> 00:49:37,650
but you also want to capture and distill something

1071
00:49:37,650 --> 00:49:40,070
about the knowledge of the representation itself?

1072
00:49:40,070 --> 00:49:43,390
So you might want to, for example,

1073
00:49:43,390 --> 00:49:46,490
match the embeddings at some intermediate point

1074
00:49:46,490 --> 00:49:49,650
within your model using contrastive training.

1075
00:49:49,650 --> 00:49:53,390
So as opposed to just matching the logits or the output scores,

1076
00:49:53,390 --> 00:49:57,530
you can actually think about distillation specifically using

1077
00:49:57,530 --> 00:50:01,530
contrastive learning but where you want to actually supervise

1078
00:50:01,530 --> 00:50:04,635
embeddings internal to the model to be

1079
00:50:04,635 --> 00:50:06,010
kind of invariant to some viewing

1080
00:50:06,010 --> 00:50:08,770
transformation, for example.

1081
00:50:08,770 --> 00:50:10,770
So that viewing transformation-- we

1082
00:50:10,770 --> 00:50:13,150
talked about rotation invariance, et cetera, before--

1083
00:50:13,150 --> 00:50:15,050
but another version of viewing transformation

1084
00:50:15,050 --> 00:50:18,370
could be the different views of that representation

1085
00:50:18,370 --> 00:50:21,150
from the teacher, which is a big network, versus the student,

1086
00:50:21,150 --> 00:50:22,430
which is a small network.

1087
00:50:22,430 --> 00:50:22,930
Yeah?

1088
00:50:22,930 --> 00:50:24,722
AUDIENCE: So in practice, when you see this

1089
00:50:24,722 --> 00:50:26,850
and what you were talking about earlier,

1090
00:50:26,850 --> 00:50:31,282
how much smaller than the teacher is the student model?

1091
00:50:31,282 --> 00:50:31,990
SARA BEERY: Yeah.

1092
00:50:31,990 --> 00:50:33,650
That's an interesting question.

1093
00:50:33,650 --> 00:50:37,330
In practice, how much smaller than the teacher is the student?

1094
00:50:37,330 --> 00:50:42,210
So again, in my experience, it kind of depends on the data

1095
00:50:42,210 --> 00:50:44,810
that you're trying to get the model to work well on

1096
00:50:44,810 --> 00:50:47,950
and how complex it is, how complex the task is.

1097
00:50:47,950 --> 00:50:51,207
So how small you can get a model to be and still be useful

1098
00:50:51,207 --> 00:50:53,290
often is really directly related to the complexity

1099
00:50:53,290 --> 00:50:56,072
of the information you're trying to model.

1100
00:50:56,072 --> 00:50:57,530
And there's a lot of different ways

1101
00:50:57,530 --> 00:50:59,810
that people try to get small models.

1102
00:50:59,810 --> 00:51:02,790
If you want a machine learning model to work on your phone,

1103
00:51:02,790 --> 00:51:04,730
it just has to be smaller.

1104
00:51:04,730 --> 00:51:06,670
You're not going to put it on an H200--

1105
00:51:06,670 --> 00:51:10,330
an H200 scale model on your phone.

1106
00:51:10,330 --> 00:51:13,190
But so people use distillation to make small models,

1107
00:51:13,190 --> 00:51:15,850
but they also have a lot of other mechanisms for getting

1108
00:51:15,850 --> 00:51:18,690
models to be small, things like model compression

1109
00:51:18,690 --> 00:51:21,610
or quantization, for example, that are doing things--

1110
00:51:21,610 --> 00:51:24,990
or pruning, even just explicitly removing weights from a model--

1111
00:51:24,990 --> 00:51:26,990
and trying to get the model to work pretty well.

1112
00:51:26,990 --> 00:51:30,090
And then a lot of times, methods that work quite well in terms

1113
00:51:30,090 --> 00:51:32,090
of getting really small models to be really good

1114
00:51:32,090 --> 00:51:34,770
are some combination of all of the above.

1115
00:51:34,770 --> 00:51:40,530
So do some pruning, maybe do some distillation, et cetera.

1116
00:51:40,530 --> 00:51:41,570
Yeah.

1117
00:51:41,570 --> 00:51:43,690
AUDIENCE: Is that basically related to how

1118
00:51:43,690 --> 00:51:49,250
we see cloud Gemini that have different sizes of model--

1119
00:51:49,250 --> 00:51:53,530
is that the result of the techniques you talk about?

1120
00:51:53,530 --> 00:51:56,690
SARA BEERY: So you were asking about

1121
00:51:56,690 --> 00:51:59,603
if you see these different sizes of models from some

1122
00:51:59,603 --> 00:52:00,770
of these foundation models--

1123
00:52:00,770 --> 00:52:02,978
for example, the Gemini model, or some of the OpenAI,

1124
00:52:02,978 --> 00:52:07,010
like ChatGPT models-- why they have different sizes

1125
00:52:07,010 --> 00:52:09,370
and whether it's actually using distillation

1126
00:52:09,370 --> 00:52:11,410
versus just training those models from scratch

1127
00:52:11,410 --> 00:52:12,890
at that size.

1128
00:52:12,890 --> 00:52:16,890
They don't tell me their trade secrets, so I don't know.

1129
00:52:16,890 --> 00:52:18,450
But I will say the reason that you

1130
00:52:18,450 --> 00:52:22,570
see these different models put out versions that are small

1131
00:52:22,570 --> 00:52:25,370
versus big is because they know that different downstream use

1132
00:52:25,370 --> 00:52:28,010
cases will have access to different resources.

1133
00:52:28,010 --> 00:52:31,250
Often, it's kind of like-- the big models are better

1134
00:52:31,250 --> 00:52:33,930
than the small models, but you use the biggest model

1135
00:52:33,930 --> 00:52:37,540
you can afford where afford might be a physical constraint,

1136
00:52:37,540 --> 00:52:42,960
it might be a monetary constraint, speed.

1137
00:52:42,960 --> 00:52:44,560
Cool.

1138
00:52:44,560 --> 00:52:45,300
Anyway, cool.

1139
00:52:45,300 --> 00:52:48,380
So here, instead of thinking about rotations or whatever,

1140
00:52:48,380 --> 00:52:50,040
the two different views that we want

1141
00:52:50,040 --> 00:52:51,760
to be similar in this contrastive way

1142
00:52:51,760 --> 00:52:54,400
are maybe intermediate representations

1143
00:52:54,400 --> 00:52:56,680
from two different models, and then the idea

1144
00:52:56,680 --> 00:53:00,400
is that you try to get this student to output embeddings

1145
00:53:00,400 --> 00:53:02,840
or output a representation for input data

1146
00:53:02,840 --> 00:53:06,280
that's similar to the teacher's representation.

1147
00:53:06,280 --> 00:53:12,400
And there's this cool thing that you can see in the logit space.

1148
00:53:12,400 --> 00:53:16,560
So specifically, if you train these--

1149
00:53:16,560 --> 00:53:19,840
do this representation distillation,

1150
00:53:19,840 --> 00:53:24,480
and you look at the correlation coefficients between the class

1151
00:53:24,480 --> 00:53:27,800
output logits-- so essentially, the student's logits minus

1152
00:53:27,800 --> 00:53:29,640
the teacher's logits-- on CIFAR10,

1153
00:53:29,640 --> 00:53:32,280
these hundred classes by hundred classes--

1154
00:53:32,280 --> 00:53:36,360
you can see that you get this much, much cleaner behavior when

1155
00:53:36,360 --> 00:53:39,760
you do this kind of contrastive representation

1156
00:53:39,760 --> 00:53:43,960
distillation versus just doing standard knowledge distillation.

1157
00:53:43,960 --> 00:53:44,720
Cool.

1158
00:53:44,720 --> 00:53:48,400
So talking again about speed, compression versus distillation.

1159
00:53:48,400 --> 00:53:52,240
So there are a lot of different mechanisms for trying

1160
00:53:52,240 --> 00:53:54,400
to make models small.

1161
00:53:54,400 --> 00:53:56,360
So both compression and distillation

1162
00:53:56,360 --> 00:53:59,200
will give you smaller models with similar accuracy

1163
00:53:59,200 --> 00:54:01,240
to large ones.

1164
00:54:01,240 --> 00:54:04,442
Distillation will use SGD to train a smaller model

1165
00:54:04,442 --> 00:54:06,400
to match the characteristics of a large one, so

1166
00:54:06,400 --> 00:54:07,920
stochastic gradient descent.

1167
00:54:07,920 --> 00:54:12,520
That process of training can be computationally expensive.

1168
00:54:12,520 --> 00:54:16,015
Compression, using maybe pruning or quantization

1169
00:54:16,015 --> 00:54:17,640
to try to reduce the computational cost

1170
00:54:17,640 --> 00:54:20,925
of a large model, that can be much more efficient

1171
00:54:20,925 --> 00:54:22,300
from a computational perspective.

1172
00:54:22,300 --> 00:54:25,280
So sometimes, people want to use compression

1173
00:54:25,280 --> 00:54:28,280
over distillation just because of the speed

1174
00:54:28,280 --> 00:54:32,120
or the computational cost of training.

1175
00:54:32,120 --> 00:54:37,320
So here is an example from a pretty classic work called

1176
00:54:37,320 --> 00:54:40,720
optimal brain compression that's essentially doing

1177
00:54:40,720 --> 00:54:42,775
both quantization and pruning.

1178
00:54:42,775 --> 00:54:44,400
And here, they're kind of getting a bit

1179
00:54:44,400 --> 00:54:46,120
of the best of both worlds.

1180
00:54:46,120 --> 00:54:50,440
So pruning is super computationally efficient

1181
00:54:50,440 --> 00:54:53,400
because you're just deleting stuff.

1182
00:54:53,400 --> 00:54:56,960
Quantization can be a little bit more expensive

1183
00:54:56,960 --> 00:54:59,792
and can also explicitly have some components that

1184
00:54:59,792 --> 00:55:02,000
feel similar to distillation because you're basically

1185
00:55:02,000 --> 00:55:05,520
trying to say, I want intermediate representations

1186
00:55:05,520 --> 00:55:07,840
to be similar.

1187
00:55:07,840 --> 00:55:09,760
And so here, they might come up with some

1188
00:55:09,760 --> 00:55:13,120
of interesting combination of a mask

1189
00:55:13,120 --> 00:55:17,920
where you're kind of getting efficient, global optimal

1190
00:55:17,920 --> 00:55:21,320
compression in some way, but without it actually being

1191
00:55:21,320 --> 00:55:22,880
computationally expensive.

1192
00:55:22,880 --> 00:55:25,820
Why do you want compression that isn't computationally expensive?

1193
00:55:25,820 --> 00:55:29,200
Again, because it's cool if you can actually directly compress

1194
00:55:29,200 --> 00:55:31,818
a model on something like a device.

1195
00:55:31,818 --> 00:55:33,360
So maybe you don't want to have to do

1196
00:55:33,360 --> 00:55:34,568
the compression on the cloud.

1197
00:55:34,568 --> 00:55:37,640
You actually need to do the compression on the edge.

1198
00:55:37,640 --> 00:55:41,040
And then you can actually get things to be even smaller

1199
00:55:41,040 --> 00:55:45,040
and maintain accuracy if you let them specialize

1200
00:55:45,040 --> 00:55:48,220
to a specific distribution while you're compressing them.

1201
00:55:48,220 --> 00:55:50,485
So maybe you have a large scale generalist model,

1202
00:55:50,485 --> 00:55:52,360
and what you need is a small scale specialist

1203
00:55:52,360 --> 00:55:56,760
model-- so for example, a model that only knows about sharks.

1204
00:55:56,760 --> 00:55:59,320
We were able to show that you can really

1205
00:55:59,320 --> 00:56:03,740
increase the quantization, the amount that you can remove,

1206
00:56:03,740 --> 00:56:06,240
and maintain accuracy if you're letting the model specialize

1207
00:56:06,240 --> 00:56:06,920
as well.

1208
00:56:06,920 --> 00:56:08,905
And that seems pretty intuitive.

1209
00:56:08,905 --> 00:56:10,280
If you don't need to keep as much

1210
00:56:10,280 --> 00:56:14,520
of the original information, you can make the model much smaller.

1211
00:56:14,520 --> 00:56:16,160
Cool.

1212
00:56:16,160 --> 00:56:23,200
So towards the end, we're going to dig into foundation models.

1213
00:56:23,200 --> 00:56:27,040
So foundation models is a term that

1214
00:56:27,040 --> 00:56:32,950
was coined by the computer science department of Stanford.

1215
00:56:32,950 --> 00:56:35,950
And essentially, it's just trying

1216
00:56:35,950 --> 00:56:41,910
to make a semantic concept for this idea

1217
00:56:41,910 --> 00:56:47,070
of really useful transfer learning models, the idea

1218
00:56:47,070 --> 00:56:51,230
that you have some models that are so generally

1219
00:56:51,230 --> 00:56:55,990
useful that they end up being really effective for a bunch

1220
00:56:55,990 --> 00:56:57,990
of different downstream tasks.

1221
00:56:57,990 --> 00:57:03,630
And I think you can put different foundation

1222
00:57:03,630 --> 00:57:06,430
models in different buckets, I would say.

1223
00:57:06,430 --> 00:57:09,070
There's some foundation models that are fundamentally

1224
00:57:09,070 --> 00:57:13,950
representation learning models where then the question is,

1225
00:57:13,950 --> 00:57:17,150
essentially, do you need to fine-tune at all

1226
00:57:17,150 --> 00:57:18,670
to make the model useful?

1227
00:57:18,670 --> 00:57:22,750
And I would say as foundation models become

1228
00:57:22,750 --> 00:57:28,270
more and more general, you need to fine-tune them less and less,

1229
00:57:28,270 --> 00:57:32,550
because maybe the set of things that you are interested in

1230
00:57:32,550 --> 00:57:35,310
is more likely to be within the very, very

1231
00:57:35,310 --> 00:57:38,630
broad distribution that's covered by that original model.

1232
00:57:38,630 --> 00:57:43,590
And mostly, if a model doesn't need to be fine-tuned,

1233
00:57:43,590 --> 00:57:46,310
it becomes more accessible.

1234
00:57:46,310 --> 00:57:50,430
So this is kind of the power of something like ChatGPT

1235
00:57:50,430 --> 00:57:52,710
or something like CLIP or something

1236
00:57:52,710 --> 00:57:57,250
like some of these large scale foundational generative models,

1237
00:57:57,250 --> 00:57:59,470
for example, like image generation,

1238
00:57:59,470 --> 00:58:02,510
is that to be useful for any given end

1239
00:58:02,510 --> 00:58:04,870
user for the vast majority of things

1240
00:58:04,870 --> 00:58:06,590
that people are kind of interested in,

1241
00:58:06,590 --> 00:58:08,990
they don't need to be retrained.

1242
00:58:08,990 --> 00:58:11,230
They're kind of usable off the shelf.

1243
00:58:11,230 --> 00:58:13,630
And maybe one way you could think about foundation models

1244
00:58:13,630 --> 00:58:16,390
is like their use is directly related

1245
00:58:16,390 --> 00:58:20,830
to what percent of the things people want to do,

1246
00:58:20,830 --> 00:58:23,270
this model can do off the shelf without any

1247
00:58:23,270 --> 00:58:26,070
of specialized training.

1248
00:58:26,070 --> 00:58:26,870
Cool.

1249
00:58:26,870 --> 00:58:28,510
And I like this quote.

1250
00:58:28,510 --> 00:58:32,572
I think this either came from Phil or Bill.

1251
00:58:32,572 --> 00:58:35,030
"If I have seen further, it is by standing on the shoulders

1252
00:58:35,030 --> 00:58:35,650
of Giants."

1253
00:58:35,650 --> 00:58:37,670
It's a quote by Newton.

1254
00:58:37,670 --> 00:58:41,310
And this is a fun painting.

1255
00:58:41,310 --> 00:58:45,150
Blind Orion Searching for the Rising Sun by Poussin.

1256
00:58:45,150 --> 00:58:49,110
But I think the idea here is that the bigger the foundation,

1257
00:58:49,110 --> 00:58:54,950
maybe the further up we get as a starting point.

1258
00:58:54,950 --> 00:58:59,230
So what does this really mean?

1259
00:58:59,230 --> 00:59:02,430
Well, so this is a plot from OpenAI,

1260
00:59:02,430 --> 00:59:04,790
and it's only going up to 2018.

1261
00:59:04,790 --> 00:59:06,350
I should get a newer version of this

1262
00:59:06,350 --> 00:59:10,430
that captures how big these models have gotten since.

1263
00:59:10,430 --> 00:59:13,430
But it's really just tracking the fact that--

1264
00:59:13,430 --> 00:59:14,930
note that this is on a log scale,

1265
00:59:14,930 --> 00:59:17,110
so we're really seeing exponential growth

1266
00:59:17,110 --> 00:59:20,110
in the size of models over time.

1267
00:59:20,110 --> 00:59:22,950
This is the amount of compute.

1268
00:59:22,950 --> 00:59:25,990
So going from AlexNet to the AlphaGo model back in 2018 was

1269
00:59:25,990 --> 00:59:31,030
a 300,000 times increase in compute.

1270
00:59:31,030 --> 00:59:32,830
And now, some of the models we use

1271
00:59:32,830 --> 00:59:36,610
are in the billions and trillions of parameters.

1272
00:59:36,610 --> 00:59:40,510
So again, much, much more.

1273
00:59:40,510 --> 00:59:42,810
And these models tend to be quite generalizable.

1274
00:59:42,810 --> 00:59:47,710
These very large models are much more generally useful.

1275
00:59:47,710 --> 00:59:53,430
I think there's some interesting kind of dimensions here.

1276
00:59:53,430 --> 00:59:57,150
So one thing is if you look at just computing power usage over

1277
00:59:57,150 --> 00:59:59,950
time-- so this one goes up to 2021--

1278
00:59:59,950 --> 01:00:03,910
you can see that academia and even academia industry

1279
01:00:03,910 --> 01:00:06,350
collaboration, that computational power

1280
01:00:06,350 --> 01:00:09,590
use is staying about the same.

1281
01:00:09,590 --> 01:00:14,550
But industry, that's starting to scale pretty significantly.

1282
01:00:14,550 --> 01:00:19,150
So you could consider this a, who is building

1283
01:00:19,150 --> 01:00:20,730
these bigger and bigger models?

1284
01:00:20,730 --> 01:00:24,790
And definitely, I think industry is a--

1285
01:00:24,790 --> 01:00:29,070
well, they're often the only people who can afford it.

1286
01:00:29,070 --> 01:00:33,270
And I do just want to raise--

1287
01:00:33,270 --> 01:00:37,750
because it's something that I feel really strongly about--

1288
01:00:37,750 --> 01:00:40,190
that these foundation models aren't free.

1289
01:00:40,190 --> 01:00:43,350
So they're definitely not free from the sense of--

1290
01:00:43,350 --> 01:00:47,350
the companies are paying a ton in compute and in power

1291
01:00:47,350 --> 01:00:52,190
and in water usage to be able to train them.

1292
01:00:52,190 --> 01:00:54,070
Microsoft, I think, announced that they're

1293
01:00:54,070 --> 01:00:57,270
spending $80 billion next year on compute.

1294
01:00:57,270 --> 01:01:00,270
So that's a lot of money.

1295
01:01:00,270 --> 01:01:02,705
That's not something that nonindustry is going

1296
01:01:02,705 --> 01:01:03,830
to be able to keep up with.

1297
01:01:03,830 --> 01:01:05,310
A lot of that is that Microsoft is actually

1298
01:01:05,310 --> 01:01:07,060
the ones who are running a lot of the data

1299
01:01:07,060 --> 01:01:09,130
centers that then other groups like OpenAI use.

1300
01:01:11,790 --> 01:01:13,670
But these also have a cost for us

1301
01:01:13,670 --> 01:01:16,710
as a society in terms of their climate cost.

1302
01:01:16,710 --> 01:01:19,980
So this is from a paper that was looking at estimating the carbon

1303
01:01:19,980 --> 01:01:24,100
footprint of one 176 billion parameter language

1304
01:01:24,100 --> 01:01:27,060
model from Microsoft.

1305
01:01:27,060 --> 01:01:30,180
And they were looking at the entire--

1306
01:01:30,180 --> 01:01:31,160
they were saying, OK.

1307
01:01:31,160 --> 01:01:33,740
If we go from the entire scope of the climate

1308
01:01:33,740 --> 01:01:37,180
cost of this model, that would include things like raw material

1309
01:01:37,180 --> 01:01:40,340
extraction, so mining, the manufacturing

1310
01:01:40,340 --> 01:01:44,800
costs of the materials, the equipment manufacturing, model

1311
01:01:44,800 --> 01:01:48,340
training, model deployment, and disposal and end

1312
01:01:48,340 --> 01:01:50,318
of life of all of those hardware components--

1313
01:01:50,318 --> 01:01:51,860
they were only focused on these three

1314
01:01:51,860 --> 01:01:53,940
in the middle, so manufacturing, model training,

1315
01:01:53,940 --> 01:01:55,700
and model deployment.

1316
01:01:55,700 --> 01:01:59,340
And what they found was a huge factor, specifically

1317
01:01:59,340 --> 01:02:02,380
in the power consumption and how that relates

1318
01:02:02,380 --> 01:02:06,100
to carbon emissions, is the sustainability of the data

1319
01:02:06,100 --> 01:02:08,420
centers' energy sources.

1320
01:02:08,420 --> 01:02:12,460
So even much smaller models can have a much, much higher carbon

1321
01:02:12,460 --> 01:02:17,060
emission if they're being trained on data centers that

1322
01:02:17,060 --> 01:02:19,500
are not renewable power.

1323
01:02:19,500 --> 01:02:22,300
And I actually think that one of the benefits

1324
01:02:22,300 --> 01:02:26,100
of this compute boom has been the increased focus

1325
01:02:26,100 --> 01:02:28,340
on renewable energy and sustainable

1326
01:02:28,340 --> 01:02:31,240
power within data centers from these companies

1327
01:02:31,240 --> 01:02:35,400
because it's really well motivated by their bottom line.

1328
01:02:35,400 --> 01:02:38,900
They're paying a lot of money for this.

1329
01:02:38,900 --> 01:02:42,020
But the climate cost of these emissions

1330
01:02:42,020 --> 01:02:43,860
is something that they are not necessarily

1331
01:02:43,860 --> 01:02:45,040
going to foot the bill for.

1332
01:02:45,040 --> 01:02:48,880
And to put this into scale, I mean,

1333
01:02:48,880 --> 01:02:50,765
I think power usage and electricity

1334
01:02:50,765 --> 01:02:52,140
is one big dimension, but I think

1335
01:02:52,140 --> 01:02:55,020
water use is another really important dimension.

1336
01:02:55,020 --> 01:02:57,540
I think it was GPT-3--

1337
01:02:57,540 --> 01:03:00,740
was trained mostly at data centers in the state of Iowa.

1338
01:03:00,740 --> 01:03:04,460
And the water use in the state went up by 700%

1339
01:03:04,460 --> 01:03:06,900
while they were training the models.

1340
01:03:06,900 --> 01:03:08,540
I read an estimate recently that said

1341
01:03:08,540 --> 01:03:12,260
that about 50 queries to ChatGPT is a bottle of water.

1342
01:03:12,260 --> 01:03:16,140
And so if we think about, now, resource access globally,

1343
01:03:16,140 --> 01:03:20,460
we already know that we are running out of water.

1344
01:03:20,460 --> 01:03:23,940
And now, having these models-- and who

1345
01:03:23,940 --> 01:03:26,900
is benefiting from them being successful?

1346
01:03:26,900 --> 01:03:30,180
And then what that means about global society and access

1347
01:03:30,180 --> 01:03:35,020
to really necessary resources like water is complicated.

1348
01:03:35,020 --> 01:03:36,460
Yeah.

1349
01:03:36,460 --> 01:03:38,220
AUDIENCE: Just for people who may

1350
01:03:38,220 --> 01:03:41,647
be not aware, is the water consumption like--

1351
01:03:41,647 --> 01:03:42,480
SARA BEERY: Cooling.

1352
01:03:42,480 --> 01:03:43,230
AUDIENCE: Cooling.

1353
01:03:43,230 --> 01:03:44,930
And then is that water-- let's say--

1354
01:03:44,930 --> 01:03:47,180
I assume they're not using-- although maybe they are--

1355
01:03:47,180 --> 01:03:49,700
potable water on the way in.

1356
01:03:49,700 --> 01:03:53,060
Does it become unpotable when you use it for cooling?

1357
01:03:53,060 --> 01:03:56,120
It sounds like it's bad.

1358
01:03:56,120 --> 01:03:58,235
You're not getting the water back.

1359
01:03:58,235 --> 01:03:58,860
SARA BEERY: No.

1360
01:03:58,860 --> 01:04:02,060
Usually-- I mean, I think there's a lot of, actually,

1361
01:04:02,060 --> 01:04:04,940
research now into, can we do this type of cooling

1362
01:04:04,940 --> 01:04:07,500
with seawater, for example?

1363
01:04:07,500 --> 01:04:09,940
Or what are the effects of running this water

1364
01:04:09,940 --> 01:04:14,740
through these cooling systems on the portability of the water?

1365
01:04:14,740 --> 01:04:16,540
I'm not going to speak to that because this

1366
01:04:16,540 --> 01:04:18,700
is outside of the scope of my understanding

1367
01:04:18,700 --> 01:04:24,060
of these mechanical engineering systems or thermal and hydro.

1368
01:04:24,060 --> 01:04:28,300
But I will say, I think that there's a booming cottage

1369
01:04:28,300 --> 01:04:30,020
industry of researchers who are trying

1370
01:04:30,020 --> 01:04:34,020
to figure out how to make data centers less climate intensive.

1371
01:04:34,020 --> 01:04:35,900
But I'm also sure that all of you

1372
01:04:35,900 --> 01:04:40,780
know that we are vastly outstripping our capacity

1373
01:04:40,780 --> 01:04:43,900
in terms of things like power.

1374
01:04:43,900 --> 01:04:46,220
It's been very big in the news that Google

1375
01:04:46,220 --> 01:04:47,740
is considering reopening Three Mile

1376
01:04:47,740 --> 01:04:49,820
Island to power data centers.

1377
01:04:49,820 --> 01:04:53,020
So we're thinking about moving back to nuclear power

1378
01:04:53,020 --> 01:04:56,660
just to keep up with the AI hunger of this.

1379
01:04:56,660 --> 01:05:02,420
And from an ethical dimension, it does get complicated,

1380
01:05:02,420 --> 01:05:11,380
because the scale of resources being used versus who actually

1381
01:05:11,380 --> 01:05:14,220
is benefiting is a bit messy.

1382
01:05:14,220 --> 01:05:16,900
And I think a lot of groups or a lot of companies

1383
01:05:16,900 --> 01:05:20,540
would have you believe that these foundation models are

1384
01:05:20,540 --> 01:05:23,820
so beneficial to society that it's necessary.

1385
01:05:23,820 --> 01:05:28,660
But I am less clear on that.

1386
01:05:28,660 --> 01:05:31,180
And one of the things that I want--

1387
01:05:31,180 --> 01:05:38,900
I want the option for a climate friendly search to come back.

1388
01:05:38,900 --> 01:05:41,148
I don't want Google to run AI models every time

1389
01:05:41,148 --> 01:05:42,440
I'm trying to Google something.

1390
01:05:42,440 --> 01:05:43,940
I don't really need the description.

1391
01:05:43,940 --> 01:05:46,220
I would love to have an opt in that says no, I

1392
01:05:46,220 --> 01:05:47,485
want to go back to MapReduce.

1393
01:05:47,485 --> 01:05:49,360
That was much less computationally expensive.

1394
01:05:49,360 --> 01:05:51,180
Can you please give me the dumber search

1395
01:05:51,180 --> 01:05:53,010
that's more climate friendly?

1396
01:05:53,010 --> 01:05:54,760
So I've been talking to people about that.

1397
01:05:54,760 --> 01:05:57,020
Maybe we'll start to see it.

1398
01:05:57,020 --> 01:05:58,100
Yeah.

1399
01:05:58,100 --> 01:06:01,020
AUDIENCE: Is there any-- on that same kind of line--

1400
01:06:01,020 --> 01:06:05,700
any kind of research on how much time, like a compute time,

1401
01:06:05,700 --> 01:06:08,780
[INAUDIBLE]?

1402
01:06:08,780 --> 01:06:11,970
When I use Google, I have to google 10 things

1403
01:06:11,970 --> 01:06:13,330
before I get the answer.

1404
01:06:13,330 --> 01:06:18,410
But ChatGPT, I ask it once, and it gives me all the information.

1405
01:06:18,410 --> 01:06:21,210
SARA BEERY: So the question is basically

1406
01:06:21,210 --> 01:06:23,930
the time versus computational trade

1407
01:06:23,930 --> 01:06:30,370
off if you query ChatGPT once versus Google

1408
01:06:30,370 --> 01:06:32,450
searching 10 different things.

1409
01:06:32,450 --> 01:06:34,410
I don't know.

1410
01:06:34,410 --> 01:06:34,910
Yeah.

1411
01:06:34,910 --> 01:06:35,452
I don't know.

1412
01:06:35,452 --> 01:06:41,090
I think it depends also on how these things are architectured

1413
01:06:41,090 --> 01:06:45,770
in terms of things like global similarity search.

1414
01:06:45,770 --> 01:06:48,490
It also is, how good are you at using Google?

1415
01:06:51,530 --> 01:06:53,650
Yeah.

1416
01:06:53,650 --> 01:06:55,730
The thing I would really love to see

1417
01:06:55,730 --> 01:06:58,610
is more transparency about all of this

1418
01:06:58,610 --> 01:07:00,250
from the side of companies.

1419
01:07:00,250 --> 01:07:02,730
What actually is the computational cost relative

1420
01:07:02,730 --> 01:07:04,410
of these different things?

1421
01:07:04,410 --> 01:07:06,710
And a lot of companies are looking at, for example,

1422
01:07:06,710 --> 01:07:10,650
making use of these different sizes of models

1423
01:07:10,650 --> 01:07:14,290
and getting really creative about routing queries

1424
01:07:14,290 --> 01:07:17,783
to the less expensive models instead of the bigger ones,

1425
01:07:17,783 --> 01:07:18,950
unless they're more complex.

1426
01:07:18,950 --> 01:07:21,770
How do you be efficient with it?

1427
01:07:21,770 --> 01:07:23,690
But yeah, I think--

1428
01:07:23,690 --> 01:07:24,570
yeah.

1429
01:07:24,570 --> 01:07:29,190
I think there's a lot of room to improve still.

1430
01:07:29,190 --> 01:07:29,690
Cool.

1431
01:07:29,690 --> 01:07:31,710
So what does it take to train one of these models?

1432
01:07:31,710 --> 01:07:33,150
These are, again, a little bit out of date.

1433
01:07:33,150 --> 01:07:34,590
We have much bigger models now.

1434
01:07:34,590 --> 01:07:37,990
But Dalle-2 required 650 million training examples,

1435
01:07:37,990 --> 01:07:39,890
semi-curated text to image pairs.

1436
01:07:39,890 --> 01:07:44,650
GPT-3 cost estimated around $4.6 million to train once.

1437
01:07:44,650 --> 01:07:46,450
I'm sure you guys can imagine that we

1438
01:07:46,450 --> 01:07:47,970
don't train these models once.

1439
01:07:47,970 --> 01:07:50,070
We have to do some hyperparameter searching.

1440
01:07:50,070 --> 01:07:53,610
There's like some differences in comparison.

1441
01:07:53,610 --> 01:07:55,710
The PaLM model had 67 authors.

1442
01:07:55,710 --> 01:07:58,390
That's 67 engineers working on this.

1443
01:07:58,390 --> 01:08:01,050
The numbers are increasing.

1444
01:08:01,050 --> 01:08:06,010
But the argument, I guess, is these numbers

1445
01:08:06,010 --> 01:08:07,730
might be small if we think of the result

1446
01:08:07,730 --> 01:08:09,570
as foundational infrastructure.

1447
01:08:09,570 --> 01:08:11,930
So if you compare this maybe to the cost of building

1448
01:08:11,930 --> 01:08:16,390
an interstate highway system, or the cost of developing OS X,

1449
01:08:16,390 --> 01:08:17,890
maybe there's different ways that we

1450
01:08:17,890 --> 01:08:20,710
should be thinking about what the cost is

1451
01:08:20,710 --> 01:08:23,550
if this is going to be fundamentally infrastructure

1452
01:08:23,550 --> 01:08:27,090
that is foundational.

1453
01:08:27,090 --> 01:08:31,210
And one of the motivations for that perspective is that if we

1454
01:08:31,210 --> 01:08:37,149
look at scaling, as you go up in these different sizes of models,

1455
01:08:37,149 --> 01:08:40,830
and you look at the number of model parameters,

1456
01:08:40,830 --> 01:08:44,450
and you look at some metric of performance on a bunch

1457
01:08:44,450 --> 01:08:47,529
of different tasks-- so 58 different tasks--

1458
01:08:47,529 --> 01:08:49,850
what you start to see is that you really

1459
01:08:49,850 --> 01:08:53,810
don't start to see model performance get significantly

1460
01:08:53,810 --> 01:08:56,752
better, where maybe it's, quote, unquote,

1461
01:08:56,752 --> 01:08:58,210
working as well as an average human

1462
01:08:58,210 --> 01:09:01,290
on some set of these tasks, until you have

1463
01:09:01,290 --> 01:09:04,850
much, much larger model sizes.

1464
01:09:04,850 --> 01:09:06,950
My argument would be, OK.

1465
01:09:06,950 --> 01:09:08,609
Now, let's do research into efficiency

1466
01:09:08,609 --> 01:09:10,662
to get these back down.

1467
01:09:10,662 --> 01:09:12,370
So what does that actually mean if you're

1468
01:09:12,370 --> 01:09:13,750
thinking about learning foundation models?

1469
01:09:13,750 --> 01:09:15,229
So one, you have tons of data.

1470
01:09:15,229 --> 01:09:16,510
You have some learner.

1471
01:09:16,510 --> 01:09:18,677
And then you're generating all these different types

1472
01:09:18,677 --> 01:09:23,170
of models that, in many cases, are kind of ready to use.

1473
01:09:23,170 --> 01:09:27,569
So now, you want to use or adapt those foundations

1474
01:09:27,569 --> 01:09:30,810
with little or no data to solve new problems.

1475
01:09:30,810 --> 01:09:33,729
So you now might have some adapter, which

1476
01:09:33,729 --> 01:09:35,753
maybe could be something like an AI agent,

1477
01:09:35,753 --> 01:09:38,170
or it could just be something that's quite simple, where--

1478
01:09:38,170 --> 01:09:38,670
OK.

1479
01:09:38,670 --> 01:09:39,870
We just pull in CLIP.

1480
01:09:39,870 --> 01:09:41,189
We have little to no data.

1481
01:09:41,189 --> 01:09:43,569
You use your adapter, and you generate some app.

1482
01:09:43,569 --> 01:09:49,330
And I would argue that this is a lot of the most recent set

1483
01:09:49,330 --> 01:09:52,890
of startups in Silicon Valley have been some version of this,

1484
01:09:52,890 --> 01:09:55,298
this "just ask" mentality, where it's like, OK.

1485
01:09:55,298 --> 01:09:57,090
We take one of these large language models,

1486
01:09:57,090 --> 01:09:58,923
and then we come up with a really clever way

1487
01:09:58,923 --> 01:10:01,610
to ask it to do stuff for us.

1488
01:10:01,610 --> 01:10:04,450
And it's not really machine learning anymore.

1489
01:10:04,450 --> 01:10:07,680
It then turns into this kind of new paradigm of research

1490
01:10:07,680 --> 01:10:10,960
that people call prompt engineering.

1491
01:10:10,960 --> 01:10:12,380
So what does this maybe look like?

1492
01:10:12,380 --> 01:10:15,480
So here, you have something like GPT-3

1493
01:10:15,480 --> 01:10:16,980
at a very, very surface level.

1494
01:10:16,980 --> 01:10:20,060
It's trained autoregressively to predict the next character,

1495
01:10:20,060 --> 01:10:23,520
so colorless green ideas sleep, furiously.

1496
01:10:23,520 --> 01:10:26,400
So now, the adapter, again, becomes this idea

1497
01:10:26,400 --> 01:10:27,580
of prompt engineering.

1498
01:10:27,580 --> 01:10:29,080
So you say, OK.

1499
01:10:29,080 --> 01:10:30,180
Here's a review.

1500
01:10:30,180 --> 01:10:32,820
Now, you tell it to fill in the blanks.

1501
01:10:32,820 --> 01:10:35,160
The sentiment in this review is blank.

1502
01:10:35,160 --> 01:10:37,500
And it will fill in the blanks and tell you positive.

1503
01:10:37,500 --> 01:10:41,280
And I'm sure many of us have played with the capabilities

1504
01:10:41,280 --> 01:10:42,280
of these models.

1505
01:10:42,280 --> 01:10:43,560
They are shockingly good.

1506
01:10:43,560 --> 01:10:45,280
They get better all the time.

1507
01:10:45,280 --> 01:10:47,080
You can ask it dumb stuff like, what

1508
01:10:47,080 --> 01:10:49,320
are the top 10 dishes at a specific restaurant

1509
01:10:49,320 --> 01:10:50,400
in Cambridge?

1510
01:10:50,400 --> 01:10:52,520
And it will give you a list of dishes

1511
01:10:52,520 --> 01:10:55,460
and rank them based on who knows what?

1512
01:10:58,200 --> 01:11:00,920
If you say, OK.

1513
01:11:00,920 --> 01:11:04,920
Here's a list of primary numbers, and ask it to continue,

1514
01:11:04,920 --> 01:11:07,743
it will continue that list of primary numbers.

1515
01:11:07,743 --> 01:11:09,160
This is where it gets interesting,

1516
01:11:09,160 --> 01:11:10,760
because depending on who you talk to,

1517
01:11:10,760 --> 01:11:14,120
it's either learning complex patterns

1518
01:11:14,120 --> 01:11:16,960
and learning to mathematically reproduce them,

1519
01:11:16,960 --> 01:11:18,940
or it's learning to do pattern matching.

1520
01:11:18,940 --> 01:11:20,980
It's essentially just a kernel machine,

1521
01:11:20,980 --> 01:11:23,520
and you've just given it everything

1522
01:11:23,520 --> 01:11:24,960
it's potentially ever going to see

1523
01:11:24,960 --> 01:11:28,040
in terms of its training data.

1524
01:11:28,040 --> 01:11:30,120
And you can also think about what people

1525
01:11:30,120 --> 01:11:32,240
call in-context learning.

1526
01:11:32,240 --> 01:11:36,310
So here, that kind of outer loop,

1527
01:11:36,310 --> 01:11:38,560
which is like learning via stochastic gradient descent

1528
01:11:38,560 --> 01:11:41,960
during supervised pretaining, then the inner loop is just

1529
01:11:41,960 --> 01:11:44,880
this in-context learning where you essentially say,

1530
01:11:44,880 --> 01:11:48,720
here's some examples of what I'd like you to do, and then

1531
01:11:48,720 --> 01:11:52,440
ask it to continue to do them.

1532
01:11:52,440 --> 01:11:55,900
And this kind of starts to feel flavored, like meta-learning,

1533
01:11:55,900 --> 01:11:58,760
which we'll talk about a bit more on Tuesday.

1534
01:11:58,760 --> 01:12:01,240
And you can also think about its ability to extrapolate.

1535
01:12:01,240 --> 01:12:05,200
So here, you show a bunch of elephants that are counting up,

1536
01:12:05,200 --> 01:12:06,580
its icons, or whatever.

1537
01:12:06,580 --> 01:12:08,240
And then you ask it, OK.

1538
01:12:08,240 --> 01:12:14,160
Starting with these four cases, count, and it will do so.

1539
01:12:14,160 --> 01:12:17,360
Or it can do things like translate

1540
01:12:17,360 --> 01:12:21,280
and translate just from a few examples of English and Spanish.

1541
01:12:21,280 --> 01:12:24,340
But again, what is the model actually doing under the hood?

1542
01:12:24,340 --> 01:12:27,160
Is it learning something, or is it

1543
01:12:27,160 --> 01:12:28,600
finding the right kind of pattern

1544
01:12:28,600 --> 01:12:30,920
to match within its training data?

1545
01:12:30,920 --> 01:12:33,160
So what does it actually do?

1546
01:12:33,160 --> 01:12:36,480
How do we actually do good prompting?

1547
01:12:36,480 --> 01:12:39,360
It turns out that handcrafting good prompts can

1548
01:12:39,360 --> 01:12:41,120
be really tricky, and these models

1549
01:12:41,120 --> 01:12:44,400
are incredibly brittle to prompting.

1550
01:12:44,400 --> 01:12:53,400
So you can give it a different template and ask it very simple

1551
01:12:53,400 --> 01:12:54,400
things.

1552
01:12:54,400 --> 01:12:55,920
And the behavior of the system will

1553
01:12:55,920 --> 01:13:02,760
be really sensitive to how the question is asked.

1554
01:13:02,760 --> 01:13:04,837
And there's a funny--

1555
01:13:04,837 --> 01:13:06,920
someone said that it was a little bit like working

1556
01:13:06,920 --> 01:13:09,520
with a genie in a lamp.

1557
01:13:09,520 --> 01:13:12,660
You're like, if you asked the genie for no pain on Earth,

1558
01:13:12,660 --> 01:13:14,520
it might kill everybody.

1559
01:13:14,520 --> 01:13:18,480
You have to be very careful about doing

1560
01:13:18,480 --> 01:13:19,740
this type of prompting.

1561
01:13:19,740 --> 01:13:21,680
And then one of the things that people do

1562
01:13:21,680 --> 01:13:23,140
is they think about ensembling.

1563
01:13:23,140 --> 01:13:25,700
OK, we'll prompt the model with a bunch of different things,

1564
01:13:25,700 --> 01:13:27,367
and then we'll ensemble the predictions.

1565
01:13:27,367 --> 01:13:31,240
Of course, that's really computationally expensive.

1566
01:13:31,240 --> 01:13:33,000
So there's a bunch of research now into,

1567
01:13:33,000 --> 01:13:36,960
how do we actually prompt these foundation models effectively?

1568
01:13:36,960 --> 01:13:40,720
One is this idea of soft prompting, which basically looks

1569
01:13:40,720 --> 01:13:45,840
at trying to optimize some of small continuous task-specific

1570
01:13:45,840 --> 01:13:50,280
vector, like encoding, while the language or the model parameters

1571
01:13:50,280 --> 01:13:51,260
are frozen.

1572
01:13:51,260 --> 01:13:54,240
So here, it's like you have some objective.

1573
01:13:54,240 --> 01:13:57,705
It's now kind of back to the normal learning framework,

1574
01:13:57,705 --> 01:13:59,080
but we're essentially just trying

1575
01:13:59,080 --> 01:14:03,920
to find the optimal words to use as the input to the model.

1576
01:14:03,920 --> 01:14:07,000
And so this paper specifically looks at fine-tuning

1577
01:14:07,000 --> 01:14:10,640
these input embeddings, these little specializer tokens,

1578
01:14:10,640 --> 01:14:12,740
that get prepended to your input task,

1579
01:14:12,740 --> 01:14:15,360
and then they help you optimize the result

1580
01:14:15,360 --> 01:14:16,760
for some task at hand.

1581
01:14:16,760 --> 01:14:18,840
So the tokens might be somewhat gibberish,

1582
01:14:18,840 --> 01:14:20,372
but they're really good at finding

1583
01:14:20,372 --> 01:14:22,080
the place in the distribution, or helping

1584
01:14:22,080 --> 01:14:24,620
the model do that pattern matching

1585
01:14:24,620 --> 01:14:28,800
so that it gets you close to something that's effective.

1586
01:14:28,800 --> 01:14:35,680
There's also this idea of standard prompting, which

1587
01:14:35,680 --> 01:14:38,448
is like, here's a question and an answer,

1588
01:14:38,448 --> 01:14:39,740
and then here's a new question.

1589
01:14:39,740 --> 01:14:41,480
And then you ask the model to answer.

1590
01:14:41,480 --> 01:14:44,200
And then what chain-of-thought prompting does

1591
01:14:44,200 --> 01:14:47,680
is it essentially demonstrates an answer that

1592
01:14:47,680 --> 01:14:49,440
has reasoning in it.

1593
01:14:49,440 --> 01:14:51,780
So you basically tell the model that when you answer,

1594
01:14:51,780 --> 01:14:54,880
you want it to answer with logic,

1595
01:14:54,880 --> 01:14:57,000
and it's more likely that it will give you

1596
01:14:57,000 --> 01:14:58,450
an answer that has some logic.

1597
01:14:58,450 --> 01:15:00,590
And then it's been shown that the answer is

1598
01:15:00,590 --> 01:15:02,110
more likely to be right.

1599
01:15:02,110 --> 01:15:05,430
There's also really dumb stuff you can do, which is like, OK,

1600
01:15:05,430 --> 01:15:06,770
you are very smart.

1601
01:15:06,770 --> 01:15:09,070
And then ask it a question.

1602
01:15:09,070 --> 01:15:11,290
Tell the model, you're a scientist.

1603
01:15:11,290 --> 01:15:14,403
Now, tell me-- which is really funny.

1604
01:15:14,403 --> 01:15:17,070
And it's one of the reasons that people start anthropomorphizing

1605
01:15:17,070 --> 01:15:17,630
these things.

1606
01:15:17,630 --> 01:15:20,950
It's like, why do you need to give it compliments?

1607
01:15:20,950 --> 01:15:23,910
But actually, I think it's really

1608
01:15:23,910 --> 01:15:26,223
about finding the right part of the distribution.

1609
01:15:26,223 --> 01:15:28,390
You want to find the part of the distribution that's

1610
01:15:28,390 --> 01:15:32,190
learned from text, that's maybe from scientists, and maybe more

1611
01:15:32,190 --> 01:15:33,610
likely to be correct.

1612
01:15:33,610 --> 01:15:35,610
Anyway, this type of chain-of-thought prompting,

1613
01:15:35,610 --> 01:15:39,270
we're telling it how to answer, was

1614
01:15:39,270 --> 01:15:45,090
shown to be better using than standard prompting by a lot

1615
01:15:45,090 --> 01:15:47,110
and better than the prior best on these types

1616
01:15:47,110 --> 01:15:50,390
of math word problems.

1617
01:15:50,390 --> 01:15:54,310
And then CLIP is also a foundation model.

1618
01:15:54,310 --> 01:15:55,790
And so this is contrastive learning

1619
01:15:55,790 --> 01:15:58,870
where you're learning semantic similarity between text

1620
01:15:58,870 --> 01:15:59,930
and images.

1621
01:15:59,930 --> 01:16:01,430
And so one thing you could do is you

1622
01:16:01,430 --> 01:16:04,410
could treat this CLIP model as a representation learning model.

1623
01:16:04,410 --> 01:16:08,110
So the adapter could be a linear classifier built

1624
01:16:08,110 --> 01:16:09,530
on top of this image encodings.

1625
01:16:09,530 --> 01:16:12,810
And they have shown that you can do that.

1626
01:16:12,810 --> 01:16:16,230
So this is the number of labeled images per category.

1627
01:16:16,230 --> 01:16:20,150
But you can also think of it as an adapter where, now, you

1628
01:16:20,150 --> 01:16:21,710
just ask.

1629
01:16:21,710 --> 01:16:26,190
So instead of saying, let's do some fine-tuning

1630
01:16:26,190 --> 01:16:31,470
over the set of categories I care about, you can just say,

1631
01:16:31,470 --> 01:16:36,070
find me the text that is most similar to this image that's

1632
01:16:36,070 --> 01:16:38,290
of the form a photo of a-- whatever.

1633
01:16:38,290 --> 01:16:44,990
And this is where this stuff gets really weird.

1634
01:16:44,990 --> 01:16:50,230
Because if you say, OK, you have the text embedding of a photo

1635
01:16:50,230 --> 01:16:54,710
of a dog versus the text embedding of photo of dog,

1636
01:16:54,710 --> 01:16:57,230
those will be different enough that it will massively change

1637
01:16:57,230 --> 01:16:59,870
the performance of this kind of zero-shot "just ask"

1638
01:16:59,870 --> 01:17:02,070
categorization on CLIP.

1639
01:17:02,070 --> 01:17:06,270
So they are very, very brittle.

1640
01:17:06,270 --> 01:17:09,750
But you can also now think about, OK.

1641
01:17:09,750 --> 01:17:11,657
I'm going to encode a sketch of a banana,

1642
01:17:11,657 --> 01:17:13,490
and I'm going to encode a photo of a banana.

1643
01:17:13,490 --> 01:17:14,850
And I'm going to show it this.

1644
01:17:14,850 --> 01:17:16,350
And I'm going to say, which one has

1645
01:17:16,350 --> 01:17:20,070
the closest cosine similarity in that joint text image embedding

1646
01:17:20,070 --> 01:17:20,770
space?

1647
01:17:20,770 --> 01:17:23,690
And that will tell me which one it matches more closely.

1648
01:17:23,690 --> 01:17:26,830
So this is more like a sketch of a banana

1649
01:17:26,830 --> 01:17:29,630
than it is like a photo of banana.

1650
01:17:29,630 --> 01:17:31,270
One of the interesting things--

1651
01:17:31,270 --> 01:17:34,790
we've been working on some of this text-based query

1652
01:17:34,790 --> 01:17:37,370
for large image databases for iNaturalist,

1653
01:17:37,370 --> 01:17:40,950
which is a really large photo repository of species data.

1654
01:17:40,950 --> 01:17:46,650
And these CLIP models are very good at simple concepts,

1655
01:17:46,650 --> 01:17:49,350
but they're really bad at composition.

1656
01:17:49,350 --> 01:17:53,490
So they're good at ors, and they're bad at ands,

1657
01:17:53,490 --> 01:17:54,527
if that makes sense.

1658
01:17:54,527 --> 01:17:56,110
And one of the reasons for that is you

1659
01:17:56,110 --> 01:17:58,190
can think of these encodings as almost a bit

1660
01:17:58,190 --> 01:17:59,670
like a bag of words.

1661
01:17:59,670 --> 01:18:05,110
So if you say, I want to find photos of a juvenile bald eagle,

1662
01:18:05,110 --> 01:18:07,030
it will find photos of juvenile things

1663
01:18:07,030 --> 01:18:08,690
and photos of bald eagles.

1664
01:18:08,690 --> 01:18:11,350
But it's not necessarily going to prioritize photos that

1665
01:18:11,350 --> 01:18:14,190
are juvenile and bald eagle.

1666
01:18:14,190 --> 01:18:15,830
So that type of compositional querying

1667
01:18:15,830 --> 01:18:17,790
is a whole other area of research

1668
01:18:17,790 --> 01:18:20,470
trying to make these models better at compositions.

1669
01:18:20,470 --> 01:18:23,390
And there was a really fun paper in the last year or so

1670
01:18:23,390 --> 01:18:25,750
that was teaching CLIP to say no.

1671
01:18:25,750 --> 01:18:27,550
This is also really bad at-- if I

1672
01:18:27,550 --> 01:18:30,030
say not a photo of a bald eagle, it

1673
01:18:30,030 --> 01:18:32,090
would just find a bunch of photos of bald eagles.

1674
01:18:32,090 --> 01:18:34,090
It's bad at the negation.

1675
01:18:34,090 --> 01:18:34,590
Cool.

1676
01:18:34,590 --> 01:18:37,630
So can you think about prompting in other modalities, too,

1677
01:18:37,630 --> 01:18:38,890
which is actually kind of fun.

1678
01:18:38,890 --> 01:18:40,790
It's just a bit of a psychological trick.

1679
01:18:40,790 --> 01:18:43,710
So here's this idea of CLIP prompting.

1680
01:18:43,710 --> 01:18:46,268
So instead of modifying the text encoder, saying, OK,

1681
01:18:46,268 --> 01:18:48,310
here's some different possible text descriptions,

1682
01:18:48,310 --> 01:18:51,670
like finding matches, you can also

1683
01:18:51,670 --> 01:18:53,990
look at mechanisms to modify the image

1684
01:18:53,990 --> 01:18:56,455
encoder with a visual prompt.

1685
01:18:56,455 --> 01:18:57,830
So here, this is an example where

1686
01:18:57,830 --> 01:19:01,310
they've learned this optimal kind of border thing

1687
01:19:01,310 --> 01:19:03,670
that they put on the image.

1688
01:19:03,670 --> 01:19:06,537
And it's kind of similar to adversarial noise.

1689
01:19:06,537 --> 01:19:08,370
We learned about learning adversarial noise,

1690
01:19:08,370 --> 01:19:11,770
but this is now like learning beneficial noise.

1691
01:19:11,770 --> 01:19:13,310
It's like, what's the thing that you

1692
01:19:13,310 --> 01:19:16,150
can add to your image that will maximize

1693
01:19:16,150 --> 01:19:20,270
the likelihood that this CLIP model will properly

1694
01:19:20,270 --> 01:19:22,550
map the correct category?

1695
01:19:22,550 --> 01:19:23,790
Which is fascinating.

1696
01:19:23,790 --> 01:19:26,750
So given a fixed model, you can learn from a data

1697
01:19:26,750 --> 01:19:28,397
set, what can you add?

1698
01:19:28,397 --> 01:19:30,230
What types of noise can you add to the image

1699
01:19:30,230 --> 01:19:31,710
to actually make the model better

1700
01:19:31,710 --> 01:19:36,670
at recognizing dogs versus other things?

1701
01:19:36,670 --> 01:19:38,910
And this is noise that helps, no matter

1702
01:19:38,910 --> 01:19:42,710
what the input image is, for some fixed data set, domain,

1703
01:19:42,710 --> 01:19:43,330
or paradigm.

1704
01:19:43,330 --> 01:19:45,750
So you have some training data, and you're basically

1705
01:19:45,750 --> 01:19:49,500
finding some constrained set of noise

1706
01:19:49,500 --> 01:19:52,280
where, if you add that noise to any image,

1707
01:19:52,280 --> 01:19:53,780
it maximally improves the prediction

1708
01:19:53,780 --> 01:19:55,500
for the correct class.

1709
01:19:55,500 --> 01:19:57,060
Kind of trippy, right?

1710
01:19:57,060 --> 01:19:57,992
It's cool.

1711
01:19:57,992 --> 01:19:59,700
But really, what it's saying is something

1712
01:19:59,700 --> 01:20:04,860
weird about the underlying statistics of the model itself.

1713
01:20:04,860 --> 01:20:10,213
So here, it's almost like you're adversarially reprogramming

1714
01:20:10,213 --> 01:20:12,380
the model, so you can also think about learning them

1715
01:20:12,380 --> 01:20:15,420
in an adversarial way where it's like, you find this thing you

1716
01:20:15,420 --> 01:20:18,540
can add to images that will make it maximally wrong in this CLIP

1717
01:20:18,540 --> 01:20:21,380
embedding space.

1718
01:20:21,380 --> 01:20:24,380
And this is a really weird version of this.

1719
01:20:24,380 --> 01:20:28,980
So here, this was an experiment where they basically

1720
01:20:28,980 --> 01:20:31,300
took a bunch of satellite imagery,

1721
01:20:31,300 --> 01:20:35,220
and they found that adding a single bright pink pixel--

1722
01:20:35,220 --> 01:20:36,720
I don't know if you guys can see it.

1723
01:20:36,720 --> 01:20:41,460
It's up there, this one little pink pixel.

1724
01:20:41,460 --> 01:20:44,900
They found that if they add a single pink pixel

1725
01:20:44,900 --> 01:20:48,100
to every single satellite image in the same place,

1726
01:20:48,100 --> 01:20:49,880
it improves performance by a lot.

1727
01:20:52,420 --> 01:20:57,420
AUDIENCE: Is that-- I mean, did they check if, in the training

1728
01:20:57,420 --> 01:21:01,220
data set for this model, it statistically was--

1729
01:21:01,220 --> 01:21:03,360
I guess you're saying it's for every class.

1730
01:21:03,360 --> 01:21:05,100
SARA BEERY: For every class.

1731
01:21:05,100 --> 01:21:08,720
But I mean, if you have a fixed model and a fixed data set,

1732
01:21:08,720 --> 01:21:10,580
then you can figure out, what is--

1733
01:21:10,580 --> 01:21:13,100
in this particular constraint, I think they were saying,

1734
01:21:13,100 --> 01:21:15,620
we want to add a single pixel-- we

1735
01:21:15,620 --> 01:21:18,740
want to change a single pixel value to a specific color,

1736
01:21:18,740 --> 01:21:20,420
find the optimal version.

1737
01:21:20,420 --> 01:21:25,620
And weirdly, it gives a 3% boost in accuracy

1738
01:21:25,620 --> 01:21:27,460
across all the categories.

1739
01:21:27,460 --> 01:21:28,020
Anyway.

1740
01:21:28,020 --> 01:21:30,700
So I think that this type of work--

1741
01:21:30,700 --> 01:21:32,557
this is not saying the best thing

1742
01:21:32,557 --> 01:21:34,140
to do to make these models work really

1743
01:21:34,140 --> 01:21:36,360
well is do one of these weird tricks like this.

1744
01:21:36,360 --> 01:21:40,260
I think it's more enlightening about what the heck

1745
01:21:40,260 --> 01:21:43,260
these models are learning, how fragile and complex

1746
01:21:43,260 --> 01:21:47,020
the statistical representations are.

1747
01:21:47,020 --> 01:21:49,460
And you can think about this adaptation happening

1748
01:21:49,460 --> 01:21:52,220
on the inputs, on the mapping, or on the outputs, this type

1749
01:21:52,220 --> 01:21:54,420
of prompt-based adaptation.

1750
01:21:54,420 --> 01:21:57,860
So you can take these pretained models,

1751
01:21:57,860 --> 01:21:59,740
and you can do fine-tuning.

1752
01:21:59,740 --> 01:22:02,220
You can take pretained models, and you can just

1753
01:22:02,220 --> 01:22:05,280
learn some linear layer that does some reprojection.

1754
01:22:05,280 --> 01:22:07,020
Or you can try and do this type of visual

1755
01:22:07,020 --> 01:22:10,460
prompting where you're explicitly adding something

1756
01:22:10,460 --> 01:22:13,420
to the top of an image, and then now, it's

1757
01:22:13,420 --> 01:22:15,700
getting a better prediction.

1758
01:22:15,700 --> 01:22:18,260
And there's a bunch of interesting stuff

1759
01:22:18,260 --> 01:22:21,380
where people show that you can do this visual prompt tuning

1760
01:22:21,380 --> 01:22:25,380
for these really large models that can be useful even when

1761
01:22:25,380 --> 01:22:28,280
the model weights or the logits are not available.

1762
01:22:28,280 --> 01:22:29,820
It's kind of like distillation.

1763
01:22:29,820 --> 01:22:31,385
If you just add things to the input,

1764
01:22:31,385 --> 01:22:33,260
see what the output is, you don't need access

1765
01:22:33,260 --> 01:22:36,700
to the underlying model to find this type of visual prompting

1766
01:22:36,700 --> 01:22:38,880
that's valuable.

1767
01:22:38,880 --> 01:22:40,380
And then there's another fun version

1768
01:22:40,380 --> 01:22:46,380
of this, which is like fill in the blank style tuning.

1769
01:22:46,380 --> 01:22:47,980
So basically, these people figured out

1770
01:22:47,980 --> 01:22:54,700
how to get an image foundation model to do segmentation

1771
01:22:54,700 --> 01:22:56,720
via in-context prompting.

1772
01:22:56,720 --> 01:22:59,180
So they made these little grids of images

1773
01:22:59,180 --> 01:23:02,180
where they would show an image with an example

1774
01:23:02,180 --> 01:23:05,180
of a segmentation mask for a class, and then another image,

1775
01:23:05,180 --> 01:23:07,720
and then have this empty area.

1776
01:23:07,720 --> 01:23:10,180
And then they would use an inpainting foundation model

1777
01:23:10,180 --> 01:23:14,780
to get it to prompt you to do segmentation,

1778
01:23:14,780 --> 01:23:17,440
even without being explicitly trained to do segmentation.

1779
01:23:17,440 --> 01:23:19,982
And then they showed this for many different types of things.

1780
01:23:19,982 --> 01:23:22,140
So edge detection, colorization, inpainting.

1781
01:23:22,140 --> 01:23:24,660
You're essentially asking these models--

1782
01:23:24,660 --> 01:23:26,780
you're prompting them with, here's

1783
01:23:26,780 --> 01:23:30,640
an example of what I want with one case,

1784
01:23:30,640 --> 01:23:33,380
and then it's just giving you the kind of correct label

1785
01:23:33,380 --> 01:23:37,700
across a bunch of different possible downstream types

1786
01:23:37,700 --> 01:23:39,060
of categories.

1787
01:23:39,060 --> 01:23:44,530
So these foundation models really have a lot of capacity,

1788
01:23:44,530 --> 01:23:46,410
and there's so many creative ways

1789
01:23:46,410 --> 01:23:48,570
that we can try to harness that capacity that I

1790
01:23:48,570 --> 01:23:50,747
think people have looked at.

1791
01:23:50,747 --> 01:23:52,330
And there's some interesting resources

1792
01:23:52,330 --> 01:23:54,490
if you guys are interested.

1793
01:23:54,490 --> 01:23:56,630
So in the last minute-- sorry.

1794
01:23:56,630 --> 01:23:58,890
We're kind of running late today.

1795
01:23:58,890 --> 01:24:00,730
Combining foundation models.

1796
01:24:00,730 --> 01:24:04,070
So you can learn foundation models and adapt them,

1797
01:24:04,070 --> 01:24:05,850
but you can also think about taking

1798
01:24:05,850 --> 01:24:07,490
these combinations of foundation models

1799
01:24:07,490 --> 01:24:08,865
and building things that are kind

1800
01:24:08,865 --> 01:24:11,470
of nonmodular on top of, again, these building blocks.

1801
01:24:11,470 --> 01:24:14,370
So you want to do something that's text to image.

1802
01:24:14,370 --> 01:24:17,110
You can take VQGAN and CLIP.

1803
01:24:17,110 --> 01:24:19,410
You can plug pretained models together.

1804
